WEBVTT

1
00:00:24.867 --> 00:00:27.405
After hours of getting
this thing right...

2
00:00:34.001 --> 00:00:35.833
God, look at that.
Look, I'm on television.

3
00:00:35.962 --> 00:00:38.545
- Hey! Isn't that amazing?
- Yeah, it is.

4
00:00:38.673 --> 00:00:40.710
- You're on TV in New York, too.
- What's that?

5
00:00:40.841 --> 00:00:42.377
- You're on TV in New York, too.
- No, no.

6
00:00:42.552 --> 00:00:44.760
- Yes, you are.
- Am I really? Are you serious?

7
00:00:44.887 --> 00:00:47.129
- Yeah, they got you in New York.
- God.

8
00:00:51.143 --> 00:00:53.305
I'm gonna let you
put it in your own ear.

9
00:00:53.437 --> 00:00:54.644
- Really?
- It's a talk back.

10
00:00:54.772 --> 00:00:58.561
- They're going to talk to you.
- This is not the real thing, right?

11
00:00:58.693 --> 00:01:00.025
You just want a picture of me now?

12
00:01:00.194 --> 00:01:02.902
- They're going to sit you here first.
- God.

13
00:01:06.909 --> 00:01:08.741
You need to tell me
where the restroom is, too,

14
00:01:08.869 --> 00:01:10.155
cos I'm deathly ill, actually,

15
00:01:10.329 --> 00:01:11.911
and ready to throw up
at any moment, so...

16
00:01:12.039 --> 00:01:16.374
- It's right across the hall.
- Great. I'm not joking.

17
00:01:24.385 --> 00:01:27.628
We're ready to go, gentlemen.
New York's waiting for a shot of him.

18
00:01:58.753 --> 00:02:02.463
If you see in my eyes,
I've been crying just a little bit.

19
00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:05.628
And it seems really ridiculous
because I've never met the man.

20
00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:11.552
I know life is ephemeral,
but I just, you know,

21
00:02:11.682 --> 00:02:14.595
I expected him to be around
a little longer.

22
00:02:14.727 --> 00:02:17.014
Pretty sure everybody did,
but, you know...

23
00:02:17.146 --> 00:02:21.140
The thing I'm using right now,
an iMac, he made.

24
00:02:21.275 --> 00:02:25.019
He made the iMac.
He made the Macbook.

25
00:02:25.154 --> 00:02:28.397
He made the Macbook Pro.
He made the Macbook Air.

26
00:02:28.532 --> 00:02:32.196
He made the iPhone.
He made the iPod.

27
00:02:34.038 --> 00:02:38.783
Yeah, he's made the iPod Touch.
He's made everything.

28
00:02:44.298 --> 00:02:49.544
<i>Hey, Mr Tambourine Man
Play a song for me</i>

29
00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:54.681
<i>I'm not sleepy
And there's no place I'm going to</i>

30
00:02:55.643 --> 00:03:01.480
<i>Hey, Mr Tambourine Man
Play a song for me</i>

31
00:03:01.607 --> 00:03:06.898
<i>In the jingle jangle morning
I'll come following you</i>

32
00:03:07.905 --> 00:03:12.070
It's not often that the whole
planet seems to feel a loss together,

33
00:03:12.201 --> 00:03:14.067
but after the death of Steve Jobs,

34
00:03:14.203 --> 00:03:17.162
co-founder of Apple
and singular dreamer,

35
00:03:17.289 --> 00:03:20.373
all day, we watched
as there was a kind of global wake.

36
00:03:20.501 --> 00:03:24.541
<i>On Facebook, millions changing
their profiles to the Apple logo.</i>

37
00:03:24.672 --> 00:03:28.165
<i>A kind of black armband,
a gesture of gratitude.</i>

38
00:03:28.384 --> 00:03:32.549
<i>We've been monitoring
the hashtag "thankyousteve."</i>

39
00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.387
<i>My favorite tweet last night</i>

40
00:03:34.598 --> 00:03:39.184
<i>was four simple letters
simply saying, "iSad."</i>

41
00:03:52.575 --> 00:03:53.861
Hi.

42
00:03:57.997 --> 00:04:01.161
<i>When Steve Jobs died,
I was mystified.</i>

43
00:04:01.292 --> 00:04:05.081
<i>What accounted for the grief of
millions of people who didn't know him?</i>

44
00:04:05.212 --> 00:04:08.580
<i>I'd seen it with John Lennon
and Martin Luther king,</i>

45
00:04:08.716 --> 00:04:12.300
<i>but Steve Jobs wasn't a singer
or a civil-rights leader.</i>

46
00:04:13.512 --> 00:04:16.676
Many commentators
were surprised

47
00:04:16.807 --> 00:04:21.893
by the intensity and the power
of this wave of emotion.

48
00:04:22.021 --> 00:04:27.016
What was it?
And I think it was truly love.

49
00:04:27.151 --> 00:04:33.193
Jobs has proven to be the one
and only person in the world

50
00:04:33.324 --> 00:04:38.615
who can create
technology products that people love.

51
00:04:41.248 --> 00:04:42.910
Wall-E.

52
00:04:43.793 --> 00:04:47.207
<i>I love "Wall-E,"
a film Jobs's Pixar produced,</i>

53
00:04:47.338 --> 00:04:49.546
<i>and I love my iPhone,</i>

54
00:04:49.757 --> 00:04:54.047
<i>but the grief for Jobs seemed to go
beyond the products he left behind.</i>

55
00:04:54.178 --> 00:04:57.592
<i>We mourned the man himself,
but why?</i>

56
00:04:57.723 --> 00:05:01.888
<i>Behind the scenes, Jobs could be
ruthless, deceitful and cruel.</i>

57
00:05:02.019 --> 00:05:07.310
<i>Yet he won our hearts by convincing us
that Apple represented a higher ideal.</i>

58
00:05:07.441 --> 00:05:11.685
<i>It was not like other companies.
It was different.</i>

59
00:05:13.739 --> 00:05:18.609
Good morning and welcome to Apple's
1984 annual shareholders' meeting.

60
00:05:18.744 --> 00:05:21.157
I'd like to open the meeting
with part of an old poem,

61
00:05:21.288 --> 00:05:24.452
about a 20-year-old poem, by Dylan.
That's Bob Dylan.

62
00:05:26.502 --> 00:05:29.711
"Come writers and critics
who prophesize with your pens

63
00:05:29.839 --> 00:05:32.422
and keep your eyes wide,
the chance won't come again."

64
00:05:32.591 --> 00:05:35.299
"And don't speak too soon
for the wheel's still in spin

65
00:05:35.469 --> 00:05:37.836
and there's no telling
who that it's naming."

66
00:05:37.972 --> 00:05:42.717
"For the loser now will be later to win,
for the times, they are a-changing."

67
00:05:42.935 --> 00:05:46.849
<i>Jobs loved Dylan
maybe because he wasn’t just one thing.</i>

68
00:05:47.022 --> 00:05:51.437
<i>He was a storyteller who could
be whatever we wanted him to be.</i>

69
00:05:52.653 --> 00:05:55.987
I don't even what know what
<i>All Along The Watchtower</i> means.

70
00:05:56.115 --> 00:05:58.402
I think it is one of
the most beautiful, haunting,

71
00:05:58.534 --> 00:06:04.326
brilliant pieces of poetry ever.
And to me, it's like Steve.

72
00:06:05.875 --> 00:06:09.118
"There must be some way
out of here, said the..."

73
00:06:09.253 --> 00:06:10.253
What is it?

74
00:06:10.296 --> 00:06:11.912
<i>Said the Joker to the Thief.</i>

75
00:06:12.256 --> 00:06:13.622
He's both.

76
00:06:28.564 --> 00:06:31.932
<i>"There must be
Some way out of here"</i>

77
00:06:32.067 --> 00:06:34.024
<i>Said the joker to the thief</i>

78
00:06:35.154 --> 00:06:38.568
<i>There's too much confusion</i>

79
00:06:38.741 --> 00:06:40.573
<i>I can't get no relief</i>

80
00:06:41.952 --> 00:06:44.660
<i>Businessmen, they drink my wine</i>

81
00:06:44.788 --> 00:06:48.202
<i>Plowmen dig my earth</i>

82
00:06:48.334 --> 00:06:51.372
<i>None of them along the line</i>

83
00:06:51.503 --> 00:06:54.667
<i>Know what any of it is worth</i>

84
00:07:31.877 --> 00:07:34.039
<i>There's something going on
here in life</i>

85
00:07:34.171 --> 00:07:38.006
<i>beyond just a job and a family
and career.</i>

86
00:07:38.133 --> 00:07:40.876
<i>There's another side of the coin.</i>

87
00:07:44.348 --> 00:07:45.714
<i>It's the same thing</i>

88
00:07:45.891 --> 00:07:50.226
<i>that causes people to want to be poets
instead of bankers.</i>

89
00:07:51.939 --> 00:07:57.310
<i>And I think that that same spirit
can be put into products.</i>

90
00:07:57.444 --> 00:07:59.902
<i>And those products can be manufactured
and given to people,</i>

91
00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:02.363
<i>and they can sense that spirit.</i>

92
00:08:05.661 --> 00:08:09.120
<i>A computer is a straightforward,
everyday machine.</i>

93
00:08:09.248 --> 00:08:11.991
<i>A simple way of studying
the principle of how it works</i>

94
00:08:12.126 --> 00:08:15.244
<i>is that a computer is quite dead.</i>

95
00:08:15.379 --> 00:08:18.417
<i>It can do nothing without
someone to give instructions.</i>

96
00:08:20.384 --> 00:08:23.548
<i>When I was growing up,
computers weren't something to love.</i>

97
00:08:23.679 --> 00:08:25.466
<i>They were something to fear.</i>

98
00:08:25.597 --> 00:08:29.887
<i>They were huge, impersonal,
made by faceless corporations.</i>

99
00:08:30.019 --> 00:08:33.137
<i>But for Jobs, it was different.</i>

100
00:08:33.272 --> 00:08:36.185
I saw my first computer
when I was 12 at NASA.

101
00:08:36.316 --> 00:08:39.650
<i>We had a local NASA center
nearby. It was a terminal,</i>

102
00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:42.187
<i>which was connected
to a big computer somewhere.</i>

103
00:08:42.322 --> 00:08:45.110
This is one of the consoles
they might be using in the future.

104
00:08:45.284 --> 00:08:47.446
It looks very much like
just a regular typewriter.

105
00:08:47.578 --> 00:08:49.035
Too often the equipment of the past

106
00:08:49.163 --> 00:08:51.029
has sort of been designed
for other machines.

107
00:08:51.165 --> 00:08:52.326
They're really not for people.

108
00:08:52.458 --> 00:08:57.044
I saw my second computer a few
years later, the Hewlett-Packard 9100.

109
00:08:57.171 --> 00:08:59.959
The 9100 computing calculator.

110
00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:05.085
It was very large. Had a very small
cathode ray tube on it for display.

111
00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:08.839
And I got a chance to play with
one of those maybe in 1968.

112
00:09:08.974 --> 00:09:10.510
<i>I started going up</i>

113
00:09:10.642 --> 00:09:13.760
<i>to Hewlett-Packard's Palo Alto
research lab every Tuesday night,</i>

114
00:09:13.896 --> 00:09:18.061
and I spent every spare moment I had
trying to write programs for it.

115
00:09:18.192 --> 00:09:19.273
I was so fascinated by this.

116
00:09:21.487 --> 00:09:23.945
We have a pointing device
called a mouse.

117
00:09:24.073 --> 00:09:25.655
I don't know why we call it a mouse.

118
00:09:25.824 --> 00:09:30.194
<i>By 1968, Stanford's
Doug Engelbart, inventor of the mouse,</i>

119
00:09:30.329 --> 00:09:31.865
<i>was asking new questions</i>

120
00:09:31.997 --> 00:09:36.241
<i>about the essential nature of our
changing relationship with computers.</i>

121
00:09:36.376 --> 00:09:39.995
If in your office,
you as an intellectual worker

122
00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:42.964
were supplied with a computer display

123
00:09:43.092 --> 00:09:47.211
backed up by a computer
that was alive for you all day

124
00:09:47.346 --> 00:09:51.590
and was instantly responsible,
responsive,

125
00:09:51.725 --> 00:09:53.933
instantly responsive
to every action you had,

126
00:09:54.103 --> 00:09:56.060
how much value
could you derive from that?

127
00:09:56.230 --> 00:10:00.190
<i>We needed a guide to help us
navigate this new relationship.</i>

128
00:10:03.237 --> 00:10:08.278
<i>My whole adult life has been spent
building personal computers.</i>

129
00:10:08.408 --> 00:10:13.449
<i>So, the history of my vocation
and my avocations</i>

130
00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:16.914
<i>and, you know,
my growing up are all the same,</i>

131
00:10:17.084 --> 00:10:19.747
and it's very hard
to separate one from the other.

132
00:10:29.054 --> 00:10:31.888
<i>I come from a place called
Silicon Valley, California,</i>

133
00:10:32.015 --> 00:10:35.008
<i>and you'll find there are
a lot of electronics kits around.</i>

134
00:10:35.144 --> 00:10:39.058
<i>My electronics teacher realized
that I had a lot of computer ability</i>

135
00:10:39.189 --> 00:10:41.556
<i>that went beyond anything he could
possibly teach me in school.</i>

136
00:10:41.692 --> 00:10:45.231
He knew that as long as I was in class,
I was just going to sit around,

137
00:10:45.362 --> 00:10:47.194
playing pranks on the other students

138
00:10:47.406 --> 00:10:49.773
like wrapping little hair wires
around certain circuits

139
00:10:49.908 --> 00:10:52.150
so when they plugged in their radio,
it would blow up.

140
00:10:58.250 --> 00:10:59.832
<i>As hard as I think about it,</i>

141
00:11:00.002 --> 00:11:02.415
<i>I don't think I ever had one friend
who was not one of the tech kids.</i>

142
00:11:03.964 --> 00:11:07.457
<i>I met Woz when I was maybe
12 years old, 13 years old.</i>

143
00:11:07.593 --> 00:11:10.301
<i>He was the first person I met
that knew more electronics than I did.</i>

144
00:11:10.429 --> 00:11:14.764
And one of the things that Woz and I did
was we built blue boxes.

145
00:11:19.104 --> 00:11:20.970
<i>One day
I picked up a magazine,</i>

146
00:11:21.106 --> 00:11:25.350
and I started reading a story
about phone phreaks and blue boxes.

147
00:11:27.404 --> 00:11:29.566
<i>When phone phreaks
have a convention,</i>

148
00:11:29.698 --> 00:11:33.442
<i>as they did in the ballroom
of a seedy New York hotel lately,</i>

149
00:11:33.577 --> 00:11:38.197
<i>masks are given out at the door.
People don't give their right names.</i>

150
00:11:38.373 --> 00:11:43.289
The blue box was a little device that
put special tones into anybody's phone

151
00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:46.709
and those tones would connect you
anywhere you wanted.

152
00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:49.628
<i>Halfway through reading this,
I called Steve Jobs over</i>

153
00:11:49.801 --> 00:11:52.760
<i>and started reading it to him
over the phone.</i>

154
00:11:52.971 --> 00:11:55.839
There's a way to fool
the entire telephone system

155
00:11:56.016 --> 00:11:58.178
into thinking you were
a telephone computer

156
00:11:58.310 --> 00:12:01.303
and to open up itself and let you call
anywhere in the world for free.

157
00:12:01.438 --> 00:12:05.148
<i>You could call from a pay phone,
go to White Plains, New York,</i>

158
00:12:05.275 --> 00:12:06.766
<i>take a satellite to Europe.</i>

159
00:12:06.902 --> 00:12:09.485
<i>And you'd go around the world
and call the pay phone next door.</i>

160
00:12:09.655 --> 00:12:11.738
<i>Shout in the phone,
be about 30 seconds,</i>

161
00:12:11.865 --> 00:12:13.606
<i>it'd come out the other end
of the other phone.</i>

162
00:12:13.784 --> 00:12:17.528
And he's like, "Hello,"
There's a lag and, "Hello, how are you?"

163
00:12:17.663 --> 00:12:19.370
"I'm fine." You know?

164
00:12:19.498 --> 00:12:22.912
<i>Why, one might wonder,
would someone want to do that?</i>

165
00:12:23.085 --> 00:12:25.372
<i>To rip off the phone company.</i>

166
00:12:25.504 --> 00:12:27.666
And these were illegal, I have to add.

167
00:12:29.925 --> 00:12:33.509
<i>In college, I had a blue box
of my own. It was important</i>

168
00:12:33.637 --> 00:12:37.221
<i>because long-distance phone calls
were really expensive back then.</i>

169
00:12:38.642 --> 00:12:41.851
<i>It was also a way
of sticking it to the man.</i>

170
00:12:42.104 --> 00:12:45.723
<i>This would become an important
selling point for Jobs, too,</i>

171
00:12:45.857 --> 00:12:49.567
<i>even as he left
the technical work to others.</i>

172
00:12:49.695 --> 00:12:51.652
<i>Well, I had this blue box design.</i>

173
00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:54.067
<i>I did a trick in there
that I've never done that good a trick</i>

174
00:12:54.199 --> 00:12:55.656
<i>in any other design in my life.</i>

175
00:12:55.784 --> 00:12:59.744
<i>And Steve Jobs said,
"Hey, why don't we sell them?"</i>

176
00:12:59.871 --> 00:13:02.238
<i>You know, you rapidly run
out of people you want to call,</i>

177
00:13:02.374 --> 00:13:05.458
<i>but it was the magic that two teenagers</i>

178
00:13:05.585 --> 00:13:08.293
<i>could build this box
for $100 worth of parts</i>

179
00:13:08.422 --> 00:13:13.542
<i>and control hundreds of billions
of dollars of infrastructure</i>

180
00:13:13.677 --> 00:13:16.511
<i>in the entire telephone network
in the whole world.</i>

181
00:13:17.848 --> 00:13:22.764
We could sort of influence the world,
you know?

182
00:13:22.894 --> 00:13:24.556
Control it, in the case of blue boxes,

183
00:13:24.730 --> 00:13:27.143
but something much more powerful
than controlling.

184
00:13:27.274 --> 00:13:32.235
Influencing, in the case of Apple.
And they're very closely related.

185
00:13:32.362 --> 00:13:33.819
I really do, to this day,

186
00:13:33.947 --> 00:13:37.156
feel that if we hadn't had had
those blue box experiences,

187
00:13:37.367 --> 00:13:39.654
there never would have been
an Apple computer.

188
00:13:39.786 --> 00:13:41.948
<i>I think Jobs
was always a storyteller.</i>

189
00:13:42.122 --> 00:13:45.957
There was always this sense
that he was constructing a persona.

190
00:13:47.336 --> 00:13:50.079
<i>The first time I sat down
with him to work on a story,</i>

191
00:13:50.213 --> 00:13:52.330
he immediately asked me
if I had read

192
00:13:52.466 --> 00:13:57.586
<i>Thomas Kuhn's "The Structure
of Scientific Revolutions."</i>

193
00:13:57.721 --> 00:14:00.464
<i>I think he was assimilating
into this personality,</i>

194
00:14:00.599 --> 00:14:02.932
<i>this notion that he had found in Kuhn.</i>

195
00:14:03.101 --> 00:14:06.219
<i>The random result
that eventually creates</i>

196
00:14:06.355 --> 00:14:08.972
a paradigm shift where everybody
one morning wakes up,

197
00:14:09.149 --> 00:14:10.890
and they think the new way.

198
00:14:11.026 --> 00:14:15.145
<i>And I believe that he thought
that he was a paradigm shifter.</i>

199
00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:19.240
<i>That was part of his story. He wanted
to have a foot in both worlds.</i>

200
00:14:19.368 --> 00:14:22.952
<i>He wanted to be the renegade,
but he also wanted to be legit.</i>

201
00:14:24.998 --> 00:14:29.663
<i>This is the video deposition
of Steven P Jobs.</i>

202
00:14:29.795 --> 00:14:33.755
We are on the record at 9:22am.

203
00:14:33.882 --> 00:14:38.252
Can we just sort of briefly go over
your employment history after 1973?

204
00:14:39.888 --> 00:14:45.555
I was employed by Atari,
a maker of video games.

205
00:14:47.396 --> 00:14:51.766
- What time frame?
- I don't know. Early '70s.

206
00:15:04.287 --> 00:15:08.372
Creativity is a lot about anarchy.

207
00:15:10.961 --> 00:15:13.419
<i>I had been in the video-game
business two years</i>

208
00:15:13.547 --> 00:15:18.542
and our corporate culture
was really "work hard, play hard."

209
00:15:18.677 --> 00:15:20.760
<i>The true original sin of Apple</i>

210
00:15:20.887 --> 00:15:23.755
literally takes place
before the company is founded.

211
00:15:23.890 --> 00:15:27.474
<i>Jobs had left Reed College
and now he was back in Silicon Valley.</i>

212
00:15:27.602 --> 00:15:29.685
<i>Woz was working at HP.</i>

213
00:15:29.813 --> 00:15:31.224
<i>I was such a nerd.</i>

214
00:15:31.356 --> 00:15:34.349
<i>When I finished designing calculators
at Hewlett-Packard in the daytime,</i>

215
00:15:34.484 --> 00:15:36.191
<i>I would work on my own little projects.</i>

216
00:15:36.319 --> 00:15:38.527
<i>I saw "Pong" in a bowling alley,
and I said,</i>

217
00:15:38.655 --> 00:15:41.614
<i>"I know logic design,
and I know electronics of televisions."</i>

218
00:15:41.741 --> 00:15:46.782
<i>"I'll use my home TV, snake a wire in,"
and I built myself a "Pong."</i>

219
00:15:46.913 --> 00:15:48.620
Steve came back from Reed College

220
00:15:48.748 --> 00:15:51.331
and saw that I had built
my own <i>Pong</i> game.

221
00:15:51.460 --> 00:15:54.248
<i>And so that gave him the idea
to go down to Atari.</i>

222
00:15:54.379 --> 00:15:56.746
<i>And he went down,
and he showed them the board</i>

223
00:15:56.923 --> 00:15:58.459
<i>and he wound up with a job.</i>

224
00:15:58.592 --> 00:16:01.756
Steve came in and said,
in typical Steve Jobs fashion,

225
00:16:01.887 --> 00:16:03.719
"I'm not going to leave
until you hire me."

226
00:16:03.847 --> 00:16:10.515
<i>And I really appreciated his intensity.
He had one speed. Full on.</i>

227
00:16:10.645 --> 00:16:13.979
<i>I had one little project
that everyone kept turning down.</i>

228
00:16:14.107 --> 00:16:15.814
<i>It was a project called "Breakout."</i>

229
00:16:15.942 --> 00:16:19.026
<i>And finally I said,
"Steve, hey, do this for me."</i>

230
00:16:19.154 --> 00:16:20.440
In the back of my mind,

231
00:16:20.572 --> 00:16:26.489
I knew that Woz was coming over all
the time after working at HP all day,

232
00:16:26.620 --> 00:16:30.239
and I thought,
"OK, I'll put Steve on the night shift."

233
00:16:30.457 --> 00:16:33.495
"Woz will come over. I'll get
two Steves for the price of one."

234
00:16:33.627 --> 00:16:37.416
<i>Steve said, "Nolan Bushnell
of Atari wants another game built."</i>

235
00:16:37.547 --> 00:16:39.504
<i>But we only had four days, Steve said.</i>

236
00:16:39.633 --> 00:16:42.467
<i>When a game is made out of chips
and it's not a program,</i>

237
00:16:42.594 --> 00:16:45.758
<i>four days is, like, impossible.
This is months' worth of work.</i>

238
00:16:47.766 --> 00:16:48.802
<i>I did the entire design,</i>

239
00:16:48.934 --> 00:16:51.551
<i>and then Steve would breadboard
my design for a little while.</i>

240
00:16:52.562 --> 00:16:56.977
<i>We were up four days and nights
non-stop. Both got mononucleosis.</i>

241
00:16:57.108 --> 00:17:00.021
<i>And we got "Breakout" delivered
to Atari, and they paid for it.</i>

242
00:17:04.616 --> 00:17:07.654
<i>Later on,
Woz and I were out to dinner.</i>

243
00:17:07.786 --> 00:17:10.153
He was talking about <i>Breakout,</i>

244
00:17:10.330 --> 00:17:13.539
and I said, "Well, you know,
you guys got paid pretty well for it."

245
00:17:13.667 --> 00:17:16.284
He looked at me puzzled, and I said,

246
00:17:16.419 --> 00:17:19.583
"Yeah, I mean,
you did such a good job."

247
00:17:19.714 --> 00:17:24.800
"I think there was at least
a $5,000 bonus that you guys got."

248
00:17:24.928 --> 00:17:28.797
So, yeah, he was paid $7,000,
and he told me that we were paid $700,

249
00:17:28.932 --> 00:17:30.924
and he wrote me a check for $350.

250
00:17:32.143 --> 00:17:34.226
<i>You know, and that hurts
because we were friends.</i>

251
00:17:34.396 --> 00:17:36.558
<i>And do you do that to a friend?</i>

252
00:17:36.690 --> 00:17:39.774
<i>If he'd said, "I need the money,"
I would have said, "Take it all."</i>

253
00:17:39.901 --> 00:17:42.518
<i>I was happy to be on the project.</i>

254
00:17:42.654 --> 00:17:45.237
I think that Steve...

255
00:17:47.325 --> 00:17:50.363
...was very driven

256
00:17:50.495 --> 00:17:57.288
and would very often take
shortcuts to achieve his goals.

257
00:18:02.465 --> 00:18:06.425
Then in time we'll tell who has fell

258
00:18:06.553 --> 00:18:09.546
<i>And who's been left behind</i>

259
00:18:09.681 --> 00:18:12.924
<i>When you go your way
And I go mine</i>

260
00:18:15.937 --> 00:18:19.101
<i>Apple was a sitcom.
It was a 30-year sitcom.</i>

261
00:18:19.232 --> 00:18:21.690
And Steve was the main character.

262
00:18:25.196 --> 00:18:28.405
<i>This was written in December 1976.</i>

263
00:18:28.533 --> 00:18:32.743
In fact it starts out saying,
"Who's Apple," so that was very early.

264
00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:39.250
<i>He and Woz came in.
Steve had long hair down his back.</i>

265
00:18:39.419 --> 00:18:44.380
<i>He had a Ho Chi Minh beard,
cutoffs, Birkenstocks.</i>

266
00:18:44.507 --> 00:18:48.376
And Wozniak was maybe a little bit
upscale from that, but not much.

267
00:18:48.511 --> 00:18:50.628
I used to like Intel's advertising,

268
00:18:50.764 --> 00:18:53.757
So I called them up one day,
and I said, "Who does your advertising?"

269
00:18:53.892 --> 00:18:56.805
They said, "Well, Regis McKenna."
"What's a Regis McKenna?"

270
00:18:56.936 --> 00:18:58.768
They said, "No, it's a person."

271
00:18:58.897 --> 00:19:02.686
<i>Wozniak had a technical article
on the Apple II.</i>

272
00:19:02.817 --> 00:19:05.776
<i>He wanted us to try to get it
placed into a magazine.</i>

273
00:19:05.904 --> 00:19:10.274
Nobody could read it. It was
all technical jargon and so forth.

274
00:19:10.450 --> 00:19:12.362
<i>And so I told him
I'd have to rewrite it,</i>

275
00:19:12.494 --> 00:19:14.531
<i>and he wasn't happy about that.</i>

276
00:19:14.663 --> 00:19:16.780
He said, "No one's going to rewrite
my stuff."

277
00:19:16.915 --> 00:19:18.872
I said, "Well,
then there's nothing I can do for you,

278
00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:20.116
so you might as well leave."

279
00:19:20.293 --> 00:19:23.536
<i>Steve called back,
and he pretty much convinced me</i>

280
00:19:23.672 --> 00:19:25.789
<i>that he would be the person
that we'd be dealing with</i>

281
00:19:25.924 --> 00:19:28.883
and that Wozniak would be designing
and building things,

282
00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:30.876
which is the way it happens
in most businesses.

283
00:19:31.012 --> 00:19:32.719
The engineers are more back room

284
00:19:32.931 --> 00:19:36.675
and you work with either the
entrepreneur or the marketing people.

285
00:19:38.103 --> 00:19:40.470
<i>Did you think early on
that Steve could be the guy?</i>

286
00:19:40.605 --> 00:19:44.269
Oh, definitely. You just had to spend
a few minutes with him and you knew it.

287
00:19:44.442 --> 00:19:45.808
<i>He had the ability</i>

288
00:19:45.944 --> 00:19:49.858
<i>to talk about the possibility
of what this computer could be.</i>

289
00:19:49.989 --> 00:19:53.824
And I think the key is not just
talking about the product,

290
00:19:53.952 --> 00:19:59.038
but giving you an idea
of what is possible using this product

291
00:19:59.165 --> 00:20:02.249
and what the next generation
is going to be like.

292
00:20:02.377 --> 00:20:06.212
So he gives people this feeling
of forward movement.

293
00:20:06.339 --> 00:20:09.127
- How many calculators do you own?
- Two, maybe.

294
00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:11.963
Right, and do you use
the automatic bank-telling machines?

295
00:20:12.095 --> 00:20:14.382
- Sure.
- Life is already seducing you

296
00:20:14.639 --> 00:20:17.757
into learning this stuff.
It's not going to happen at once,

297
00:20:17.892 --> 00:20:21.306
and it's certainly not
a <i>1984-ish</i> vision at all.

298
00:20:21.479 --> 00:20:24.187
It's just going to be very gradual
and very human

299
00:20:24.315 --> 00:20:26.807
and will seduce you
into learning how to use it.

300
00:20:28.987 --> 00:20:31.980
<i>Transitioning from a hobby
to a personal computer,</i>

301
00:20:32.115 --> 00:20:34.778
<i>that whole idea was driven by Steve.</i>

302
00:20:34.909 --> 00:20:37.117
He was trying to say
we need to differentiate ourselves

303
00:20:37.245 --> 00:20:38.907
and really move out
of this hobbyist realm.

304
00:20:39.038 --> 00:20:41.906
<i>It ended up
coming out of the room saying,</i>

305
00:20:42.041 --> 00:20:44.829
<i>"We're going to call ourselves
the personal computer."</i>

306
00:20:44.961 --> 00:20:46.577
<i>Industry experts say</i>

307
00:20:46.713 --> 00:20:49.831
<i>we're no longer on the verge
of the personal computer revolution.</i>

308
00:20:50.008 --> 00:20:51.544
<i>We're right in the midst of it,
thank you.</i>

309
00:20:51.676 --> 00:20:52.883
And it's gathering steam

310
00:20:53.011 --> 00:20:56.254
with more and more people
jumping aboard every day.

311
00:20:56.389 --> 00:21:00.053
I use my computer right now
for mostly word processing.

312
00:21:00.185 --> 00:21:03.895
I use it for solar evaluation programs.

313
00:21:04.022 --> 00:21:06.139
We put our entire accounting system
on it.

314
00:21:06.274 --> 00:21:08.061
The wife can use it to store recipes.

315
00:21:08.193 --> 00:21:09.604
To balance my checkbook for me.

316
00:21:09.778 --> 00:21:11.394
We do the computer club's bulletin.

317
00:21:11.571 --> 00:21:13.153
- Playing games.
- Shopping by mail.

318
00:21:13.281 --> 00:21:15.568
- Budgeting.
- Bowling-league type scores.

319
00:21:15.700 --> 00:21:18.158
- Electronic mail.
- A guy can be creative on it.

320
00:21:18.286 --> 00:21:20.528
I mean, he can use it
for whatever he can dream up.

321
00:21:20.663 --> 00:21:22.620
This is a 21st-century bicycle

322
00:21:22.749 --> 00:21:26.618
that amplifies a certain intellectual
ability that man has.

323
00:21:26.753 --> 00:21:29.587
The effects that it's going to
have on society

324
00:21:29.714 --> 00:21:31.296
are actually going to far outstrip

325
00:21:31.424 --> 00:21:34.041
even those that the petrochemical
revolution has had.

326
00:21:34.177 --> 00:21:35.177
<i>Time</i> magazine, I think,

327
00:21:35.303 --> 00:21:39.673
said single-handedly he created
the industry because he was relentless.

328
00:21:41.142 --> 00:21:44.601
<i>The powers that be
of "Time" magazine</i>

329
00:21:44.729 --> 00:21:50.191
decided that they would make
the Man of the Year that particular year

330
00:21:50.318 --> 00:21:52.560
the Computer of the Year.

331
00:21:52.695 --> 00:21:56.063
<i>I was transferred to the bureau
in San Francisco.</i>

332
00:21:56.199 --> 00:21:59.363
And gradually I began to cotton on
to the fact

333
00:21:59.494 --> 00:22:04.034
that there were a lot of stories
in this part of California

334
00:22:04.165 --> 00:22:08.205
between San Jose
and San Francisco

335
00:22:08.336 --> 00:22:09.952
about these odd, little companies

336
00:22:10.088 --> 00:22:13.081
that people on the East Coast
at that point

337
00:22:13.216 --> 00:22:16.675
hadn't heard about
and really didn't care about.

338
00:22:16.803 --> 00:22:18.886
<i>And then I got very interested in Apple</i>

339
00:22:19.013 --> 00:22:23.724
<i>and Steve was,
of the early characters in the company,</i>

340
00:22:23.935 --> 00:22:28.976
the most articulate and the most
interesting and the oddest.

341
00:22:29.107 --> 00:22:33.021
<i>Steven Jobs helped build
the first Apple computer in his garage.</i>

342
00:22:33.152 --> 00:22:36.190
<i>He is now 26 years old
and is chairman of the board.</i>

343
00:22:37.323 --> 00:22:38.734
<i>There was some debate</i>

344
00:22:38.867 --> 00:22:40.984
<i>over whether or not
they should use the name "Apple."</i>

345
00:22:41.119 --> 00:22:43.953
<i>You know, the whole model
of the computer industry</i>

346
00:22:44.080 --> 00:22:45.742
and the computer business was IBM.

347
00:22:45.874 --> 00:22:50.665
<i>Another business service of
tomorrow made possible today by IBM.</i>

348
00:22:50.795 --> 00:22:53.412
IBM was an anonymous organization.

349
00:22:53.548 --> 00:22:56.712
No one knew who the president was.
They probably had no idea.

350
00:22:56.843 --> 00:23:02.009
<i>The IBM logo looked like it was
carved out of Roman marble, you know?</i>

351
00:23:02.140 --> 00:23:04.848
It was just
this monolithic kind of thing.

352
00:23:04.976 --> 00:23:07.844
<i>And we took just the opposite,
which was,</i>

353
00:23:07.979 --> 00:23:11.268
<i>"Let's make Steve very high profile.
Let's tell our story."</i>

354
00:23:11.399 --> 00:23:14.233
<i>Working in this garage,
Jobs and a high-school classmate</i>

355
00:23:14.360 --> 00:23:16.693
<i>quit their positions
at large electronic companies,</i>

356
00:23:16.821 --> 00:23:21.441
<i>and using tiny silicon chips,
built this small computer board.</i>

357
00:23:26.789 --> 00:23:31.409
<i>Funny, the garage story was
less of a feature in those early days.</i>

358
00:23:31.586 --> 00:23:34.124
<i>It later on became more of a look back</i>

359
00:23:34.255 --> 00:23:39.046
<i>when people started doing stories
on the background, and so forth.</i>

360
00:23:39.177 --> 00:23:45.174
You know, I told Steve this,
and most of my clients in fact,

361
00:23:45.308 --> 00:23:49.177
there's a song in <i>Fiddler on the Roof</i>
that Tevye sings.

362
00:23:49.312 --> 00:23:53.682
He says, "If I were a rich man."
And he said, "I'd sit in the temple,

363
00:23:53.816 --> 00:23:56.900
and I'd lecture to the wise men
all day long,

364
00:23:57.028 --> 00:23:58.985
and it wouldn't matter
if you're right or wrong."

365
00:23:59.113 --> 00:24:00.775
"When you're rich,
they think you know."

366
00:24:00.907 --> 00:24:02.523
So, in a technology business,

367
00:24:02.700 --> 00:24:06.535
you have to show that you are successful
in order to have a platform.

368
00:24:06.704 --> 00:24:09.242
<i>It led to
a quarter-billion-dollar business</i>

369
00:24:09.374 --> 00:24:13.334
<i>and the most popular typewriter-sized
computer on the market today.</i>

370
00:24:13.461 --> 00:24:16.249
Steve Jobs,
I realize this is your baby,

371
00:24:16.422 --> 00:24:17.958
and you've made a career out of it,

372
00:24:18.091 --> 00:24:20.549
but you're also
something of a philosopher.

373
00:24:20.718 --> 00:24:24.803
Do you see the inherent possibility
of bad coming out of all of this?

374
00:24:24.931 --> 00:24:27.264
Well, I think one of the things
you really have to look at

375
00:24:27.392 --> 00:24:30.100
is you have to go watch
some kids using these things.

376
00:24:30.228 --> 00:24:33.392
<i>And what you find is far
from something quite harmful.</i>

377
00:24:33.523 --> 00:24:35.014
<i>In effect, what you see</i>

378
00:24:35.191 --> 00:24:38.980
<i>is an instantaneous reflection
of a part of themselves,</i>

379
00:24:39.112 --> 00:24:41.570
the creative part of themselves
being expressed.

380
00:24:49.539 --> 00:24:51.997
<i>He was going for a computer</i>

381
00:24:52.125 --> 00:24:56.244
<i>that really felt like
an extension of the self.</i>

382
00:24:56.379 --> 00:25:00.293
That's what people wanted, and I think
he sensed that. He knew that.

383
00:25:01.426 --> 00:25:05.841
<i>My first book on the computer culture
was called "The Second Self."</i>

384
00:25:05.972 --> 00:25:07.929
<i>The key quote
that gave me the title was,</i>

385
00:25:08.057 --> 00:25:10.640
"When you think of a computer,

386
00:25:10.810 --> 00:25:14.850
you put a little piece of your mind
into the computer's mind,

387
00:25:14.981 --> 00:25:17.268
and you come to think
of yourself differently."

388
00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:23.021
Our whole company,
our whole philosophical base,

389
00:25:23.197 --> 00:25:27.282
is founded on one principle.
And that one principle

390
00:25:27.410 --> 00:25:32.280
is that there's something very special
and very historically different

391
00:25:32.415 --> 00:25:36.876
that takes place when you have
one computer and one person.

392
00:25:38.379 --> 00:25:40.712
<i>Did you have an opportunity
to meet Jobs?</i>

393
00:25:40.923 --> 00:25:43.165
<i>Yes,
I met him on several occasions.</i>

394
00:25:43.301 --> 00:25:45.293
And did you sense
from talking to him

395
00:25:45.428 --> 00:25:47.511
that he really did understand
what he was doing?

396
00:25:47.638 --> 00:25:49.800
I think he understood what he was doing.

397
00:25:51.017 --> 00:25:54.306
<i>He knew
he had created something intimate</i>

398
00:25:54.520 --> 00:25:57.513
<i>and that could be sold
as something intimate.</i>

399
00:25:57.648 --> 00:26:00.231
And it would be you.
I mean, it would be for you.

400
00:26:00.359 --> 00:26:04.478
It wasn't just for you. It was you.

401
00:26:10.495 --> 00:26:12.737
Can you just show me
the front of it?

402
00:26:12.914 --> 00:26:15.702
That's the part
that most people would recognize.

403
00:26:15.833 --> 00:26:19.577
This is a piece that everybody
remembers from the ads,

404
00:26:19.712 --> 00:26:23.331
from the <i>Time</i> magazine cover
with Steve holding it in his lap.

405
00:26:26.177 --> 00:26:29.420
And this is the famous beige that
we're never going to have any more of.

406
00:26:29.555 --> 00:26:31.217
He hated this even at this time,

407
00:26:31.349 --> 00:26:34.717
but we were kind of stuck with it
by the time we got there.

408
00:26:37.647 --> 00:26:40.185
It was a fun little machine.

409
00:26:44.612 --> 00:26:48.196
<i>He called me just out of the blue.
I was working at Xerox.</i>

410
00:26:49.367 --> 00:26:52.326
And I picked up the phone,
and it was Steve Jobs.

411
00:26:52.453 --> 00:26:55.946
And he said, "I hear you're a good guy,

412
00:26:56.082 --> 00:26:59.496
but everything you've done so far
is crap. Come work for me."

413
00:27:00.878 --> 00:27:05.122
<i>I told my wife at the time.
I said, "Well, what could happen?"</i>

414
00:27:05.258 --> 00:27:06.920
<i>"How bad could this be?"</i>

415
00:27:09.137 --> 00:27:11.379
I didn't realize how bad it could be.

416
00:27:15.101 --> 00:27:17.263
<i>First trip Steve ever made to Japan</i>

417
00:27:17.395 --> 00:27:20.433
<i>was to see what we could do about
getting a disc drive for the machine.</i>

418
00:27:20.565 --> 00:27:25.526
<i>And we saw the Sony disc facility
in Atsugi, Japan.</i>

419
00:27:25.653 --> 00:27:30.819
He had a lot of affection for Sony
because the Walkman was a machine

420
00:27:30.992 --> 00:27:33.029
that he just thought
was the bee's knees.

421
00:27:33.161 --> 00:27:36.074
You really feel the music
with a Sony Walkman

422
00:27:36.205 --> 00:27:39.573
<i>The Sony Walkman is
a tiny stereo cassette player</i>

423
00:27:39.750 --> 00:27:42.163
<i>with truly incredible sound.</i>

424
00:27:42.295 --> 00:27:44.753
<i>You really feel the music
You really feel it</i>

425
00:27:44.964 --> 00:27:47.377
I think it was the first product
in human history

426
00:27:47.508 --> 00:27:50.751
that went over a billion units.
That he liked.

427
00:27:51.804 --> 00:27:54.342
One of things that Steve thought
was important,

428
00:27:54.473 --> 00:27:56.135
and Jerry Manock facilitated it,

429
00:27:56.267 --> 00:27:58.680
was this is
where all the signatures are.

430
00:27:58.811 --> 00:28:02.304
And they're all the people,
the original group,

431
00:28:02.440 --> 00:28:08.983
that actually signed the machine.
There's Steve Jobs right in the middle.

432
00:28:10.114 --> 00:28:11.946
My name is over here.

433
00:28:12.116 --> 00:28:13.607
<i>Why did you do that?</i>

434
00:28:13.743 --> 00:28:17.578
Because the people that worked
on it consider themselves,

435
00:28:17.705 --> 00:28:20.163
and I certainly consider them, artists.

436
00:28:20.291 --> 00:28:22.658
These are the people that
under different circumstances

437
00:28:22.793 --> 00:28:27.003
<i>would be painters and poets, but,
because of the time that we live in,</i>

438
00:28:27.131 --> 00:28:29.248
<i>this new medium has appeared</i>

439
00:28:29.383 --> 00:28:32.547
<i>in which to express oneself
to one's fellow species.</i>

440
00:28:32.678 --> 00:28:36.046
And that's a medium of computing.

441
00:28:41.604 --> 00:28:45.223
<i>We would sit
in the temples in Kyoto,</i>

442
00:28:45.358 --> 00:28:49.728
<i>just taking off our shoes at the door
and sitting.</i>

443
00:29:03.668 --> 00:29:07.332
<i>Did he take from that any
kind of aesthetic vision, do you think?</i>

444
00:29:07.463 --> 00:29:11.582
<i>I think certainly.
A simplicity.</i>

445
00:29:13.094 --> 00:29:16.053
Just feeling that inner calm

446
00:29:16.180 --> 00:29:20.299
that's so available
at some places in Japan.

447
00:29:22.395 --> 00:29:25.934
<i>He was a very much a person
who was comfortable in silence.</i>

448
00:29:36.075 --> 00:29:40.786
Steve ruled by a kind of a chaos.
And it's easy to make chaos,

449
00:29:40.913 --> 00:29:44.827
and if you're comfortable with it,
you can use it as a tool.

450
00:29:44.959 --> 00:29:48.123
<i>And he used a vast number
of really irritating tools</i>

451
00:29:48.254 --> 00:29:52.624
to get other people involved
in his schemes.

452
00:29:52.758 --> 00:29:59.756
<i>He's seducing you, he's vilifying you
and he's ignoring you.</i>

453
00:29:59.890 --> 00:30:01.597
You're in one of those three states.

454
00:30:03.936 --> 00:30:07.976
<i>When you get a core group of,
you know, ten great people,</i>

455
00:30:08.149 --> 00:30:12.610
<i>it becomes self-policing
as to who they let into that group.</i>

456
00:30:12.737 --> 00:30:18.153
So, I consider the most important job
of someone like myself is recruiting.

457
00:30:18.367 --> 00:30:21.860
<i>Steve Jobs brought us all together
in a place that had no rules.</i>

458
00:30:21.996 --> 00:30:26.457
He's a maniac. He's a maniacal genius.
His job is to stir up everything.

459
00:30:26.584 --> 00:30:29.918
<i>Most places in life
are continuously telling you</i>

460
00:30:30.087 --> 00:30:32.295
<i>that your dreams aren't possible
or practical.</i>

461
00:30:32.423 --> 00:30:34.460
You don't want to hear that
when you're under 30.

462
00:30:34.592 --> 00:30:36.254
What you want to do is race after them.

463
00:30:36.385 --> 00:30:37.876
You ask yourself, why are you doing it?

464
00:30:38.095 --> 00:30:39.882
I'm certainly not doing it
for Steve Jobs.

465
00:30:40.014 --> 00:30:42.597
I'm doing it for what I think is
a much greater good than that.

466
00:30:42.725 --> 00:30:45.968
Everybody just wanted to work, not
because it was work that had to be done,

467
00:30:46.145 --> 00:30:48.512
but it was because it was something
that we really believed in.

468
00:30:48.647 --> 00:30:54.143
Here is how we see personal computers.
Here is how we want the world to be.

469
00:30:54.278 --> 00:30:56.770
And here's how we're going to change it.

470
00:30:56.906 --> 00:30:58.488
We have a vision
of what we want it to be.

471
00:30:58.616 --> 00:31:00.983
We want to convert people.
We want to make converts.

472
00:31:01.160 --> 00:31:04.153
<i>I felt my job at Macintosh</i>

473
00:31:04.288 --> 00:31:07.747
<i>was to make the division
work smoothly enough</i>

474
00:31:07.875 --> 00:31:11.710
<i>that we could actually get this thing
from really a mess of kids</i>

475
00:31:11.837 --> 00:31:14.545
<i>playing around with a bunch
of hardware and software</i>

476
00:31:14.673 --> 00:31:17.336
<i>into something that would be
a commercial product.</i>

477
00:31:17.468 --> 00:31:20.131
And that's what I did.
I got that machine finished.

478
00:31:29.021 --> 00:31:32.514
<i>It is now 1984.</i>

479
00:31:32.650 --> 00:31:35.484
IBM became the apparent visible threat.

480
00:31:35.611 --> 00:31:38.570
IBM wants it all,
and is aiming its guns

481
00:31:38.697 --> 00:31:43.032
on its last obstacle
to industry control. Apple.

482
00:31:43.160 --> 00:31:46.494
Will Big Blue dominate
the entire computer industry?

483
00:31:46.622 --> 00:31:50.457
The entire information age?
Was George Orwell right?

484
00:31:51.627 --> 00:31:55.462
<i>Today, we celebrate
the first glorious anniversary</i>

485
00:31:55.589 --> 00:31:58.457
<i>over the information
purification directives.</i>

486
00:31:58.592 --> 00:32:01.551
<i>That ad was again
a juxtaposition with IBM.</i>

487
00:32:01.679 --> 00:32:03.636
<i>- That's what it was about.</i>
<i>- Yeah.</i>

488
00:32:03.764 --> 00:32:06.222
<i>The people in the audience
were mindless IBM users.</i>

489
00:32:06.350 --> 00:32:08.216
<i>Yes. You know,
for Steve it was great</i>

490
00:32:08.352 --> 00:32:12.596
because he had this bad guy/good guy,
and he loved playing that role.

491
00:32:12.731 --> 00:32:16.645
<i>We shall prevail!</i>

492
00:32:37.256 --> 00:32:38.872
<i>Looking back,
behind the scenes,</i>

493
00:32:39.091 --> 00:32:42.505
<i>it's easy to see the irony in the ad.</i>

494
00:32:42.636 --> 00:32:44.548
<i>Today, Apple is Goliath.</i>

495
00:32:44.680 --> 00:32:46.137
Rolling. Rolling.

496
00:32:46.348 --> 00:32:48.214
<i>But even in 1984,</i>

497
00:32:48.392 --> 00:32:51.135
<i>when Apple cast itself
as the counterculture company,</i>

498
00:32:51.312 --> 00:32:54.771
<i>working at Apple was a lot tougher
than IBM.</i>

499
00:32:56.859 --> 00:32:59.021
<i>I think if you talk to a lot of
people on the Mac team,</i>

500
00:32:59.153 --> 00:33:05.150
they will tell you it was the hardest
they've ever worked in their life.

501
00:33:05.326 --> 00:33:07.033
Some of them will tell you
it was, you know,

502
00:33:07.286 --> 00:33:08.993
the happiest they've ever been
in their life,

503
00:33:09.121 --> 00:33:11.033
but I think all of them will tell you

504
00:33:11.165 --> 00:33:14.499
that it is certainly one of the most
intense and cherished experiences

505
00:33:14.627 --> 00:33:15.993
they will ever have in their life.

506
00:33:16.128 --> 00:33:18.962
Mm-hmm. Yeah, they did.
- So...

507
00:33:21.759 --> 00:33:23.751
You know...

508
00:33:25.429 --> 00:33:29.969
Some of those things are
not sustainable for some people.

509
00:33:35.773 --> 00:33:38.356
<i>I ended up
changing my entire life.</i>

510
00:33:38.567 --> 00:33:40.775
I lost my wife in that process.

511
00:33:40.903 --> 00:33:44.567
I lost my children in that process.
I lost...

512
00:33:44.740 --> 00:33:48.450
The whole structure of my life
was just changed forever

513
00:33:48.577 --> 00:33:52.571
by going and working on the Mac.

514
00:33:52.706 --> 00:33:54.789
Because the work
became so intense?

515
00:33:54.917 --> 00:33:56.374
The work was intense.

516
00:33:56.502 --> 00:33:58.539
The commitment needed to do it
was intense.

517
00:33:58.671 --> 00:34:01.664
<i>I would go into work
on a Tuesday morning</i>

518
00:34:01.799 --> 00:34:03.665
<i>and half the people would hate me,</i>

519
00:34:03.801 --> 00:34:05.417
<i>and I'd come back on Wednesday morning,</i>

520
00:34:05.594 --> 00:34:08.177
<i>and half the people would hate me,
but it was the other half.</i>

521
00:34:08.347 --> 00:34:10.760
<i>There were an awful lot
of prima Donnas in that outfit,</i>

522
00:34:10.891 --> 00:34:13.599
<i>so I was always in conflict.</i>

523
00:34:16.814 --> 00:34:20.524
Here's the piece you wrote.
You want to read it?

524
00:34:21.860 --> 00:34:26.400
"Steve's passing did come
as a bit of a shock for me."

525
00:34:26.532 --> 00:34:31.402
"For a bit more than three years,
1982 to 1985,

526
00:34:31.537 --> 00:34:34.245
we were together a lot of the time."

527
00:34:34.415 --> 00:34:38.500
"We made a dozen trips to Japan
together. We were close."

528
00:34:38.627 --> 00:34:41.665
"After that,
I only saw him a few times."

529
00:34:41.797 --> 00:34:44.164
"I haven't seen him in many years."

530
00:34:44.341 --> 00:34:48.676
"He was an extraordinary person in many
ways and quite normal in others."

531
00:34:48.804 --> 00:34:52.263
<i>"The outpouring of feelings
from people all over the world</i>

532
00:34:52.474 --> 00:34:57.139
<i>was a bit of a surprise to me at first,
and then it seemed natural."</i>

533
00:34:57.271 --> 00:35:00.059
<i>"He was for them
a combination of James Dean,</i>

534
00:35:00.190 --> 00:35:04.059
<i>Princess Diana and John Lennon
and maybe Santa Claus."</i>

535
00:35:04.194 --> 00:35:07.858
"What is in that bag of goodies?"

536
00:35:08.782 --> 00:35:12.651
<i>"The iPod, the iPhone
and the iPad are so personal."</i>

537
00:35:12.786 --> 00:35:17.781
<i>"They are warm in your hand.
They sing to you when you're alone."</i>

538
00:35:17.916 --> 00:35:19.373
<i>"They are caressed."</i>

539
00:35:21.879 --> 00:35:23.165
"In those three years together,

540
00:35:23.297 --> 00:35:26.256
I packed in a decade or two
of experience."

541
00:35:26.425 --> 00:35:31.796
"Steve packed in a couple of centuries
in his 56 years."

542
00:35:31.930 --> 00:35:36.470
"He did everything he wanted,
and all on his own terms."

543
00:35:36.602 --> 00:35:39.470
"It was a life well and fully lived,

544
00:35:39.605 --> 00:35:43.064
even if it was a bit expensive
for those of us who were close."

545
00:35:47.613 --> 00:35:50.071
You do have friends, you know?

546
00:35:50.199 --> 00:35:53.317
Even if they're bizarre people.

547
00:35:55.621 --> 00:35:58.489
Yes. He is.
He's one of those mythic characters.

548
00:35:58.624 --> 00:36:05.667
Yeah, and they're not that much fun
on the ground most of the time,

549
00:36:05.798 --> 00:36:09.337
but there are those moments
when suddenly...

550
00:36:11.095 --> 00:36:13.712
They're the only person
who could've ever done it.

551
00:36:13.931 --> 00:36:15.297
Right.

552
00:36:15.474 --> 00:36:19.093
- Yeah, and they change us.
- Right.

553
00:36:32.449 --> 00:36:35.783
<i>Without death,
there would be very little progress.</i>

554
00:36:35.911 --> 00:36:39.325
<i>I'm sure that life evolved
without death at first</i>

555
00:36:39.498 --> 00:36:44.835
<i>and found that without death,
life didn't work very well.</i>

556
00:36:44.962 --> 00:36:48.330
<i>Because it didn't make room
for the young</i>

557
00:36:48.507 --> 00:36:52.922
<i>who didn't know how the world was,
you know, 50 years ago,</i>

558
00:36:53.053 --> 00:36:56.763
<i>but who saw it as it is today
without any preconceptions</i>

559
00:36:56.890 --> 00:36:59.257
<i>and dreamed how it could be
based on that.</i>

560
00:37:04.106 --> 00:37:07.190
<i>The minute that you understand
that you can poke life,</i>

561
00:37:07.317 --> 00:37:09.149
<i>you can change it, you can mold it,</i>

562
00:37:09.278 --> 00:37:11.895
<i>you'll want to change life
and make it better</i>

563
00:37:12.030 --> 00:37:14.022
<i>cos it's kind of messed up
in a lot of ways.</i>

564
00:37:15.159 --> 00:37:18.243
<i>Once you learn that,
you'll never be the same again.</i>

565
00:37:23.792 --> 00:37:24.999
<i>Just be here.</i>

566
00:37:26.003 --> 00:37:30.964
<i>Don't judge, don't try,
don't stop, don't start. Just be here.</i>

567
00:37:31.091 --> 00:37:32.707
<i>It's all just enough.</i>

568
00:37:36.638 --> 00:37:39.005
<i>It's enough to know that I love you.</i>

569
00:37:42.686 --> 00:37:47.306
<i>Steve and I met two weeks into
our freshman year at Reed College.</i>

570
00:37:47.441 --> 00:37:51.651
<i>We had both happened to buy
"Be Here Now."</i>

571
00:37:51.820 --> 00:37:55.734
And it was such an unusual book.
I just wanted...

572
00:37:55.866 --> 00:37:58.984
I was carrying it around
and wanted somebody to talk to about it,

573
00:37:59.119 --> 00:38:02.362
and Steve was the one person
who also had read it.

574
00:38:09.838 --> 00:38:11.295
<i>When we went to India,</i>

575
00:38:11.423 --> 00:38:15.918
<i>we were looking for
remarkable experiences.</i>

576
00:38:16.053 --> 00:38:19.592
<i>We didn't have a guru.
We didn't have a particular school.</i>

577
00:38:19.723 --> 00:38:22.841
<i>And so we traveled around
for four months.</i>

578
00:38:22.976 --> 00:38:29.940
<i>Had some interesting experiences.
No major enlightenment experiences.</i>

579
00:38:30.067 --> 00:38:32.434
Steve's quote later was,

580
00:38:32.611 --> 00:38:35.103
"We had figured out that
we weren't going to meet somebody

581
00:38:35.239 --> 00:38:37.447
who was going to make us enlightened."

582
00:38:40.202 --> 00:38:42.410
<i>If you think about Hindu spirituality,</i>

583
00:38:42.579 --> 00:38:46.448
<i>you think of Mother Teresa
feeding the poor.</i>

584
00:38:46.625 --> 00:38:49.959
<i>That's not really the path
that Steve took.</i>

585
00:38:50.087 --> 00:38:52.750
Those weren't Steve's values.

586
00:38:55.717 --> 00:38:57.834
<i>It was the next year,
after India,</i>

587
00:38:57.970 --> 00:39:02.010
<i>when he connected
with the Zen Center in Los Altos.</i>

588
00:39:02.140 --> 00:39:08.603
<i>Zen is about clarity,
simplicity, cleanliness.</i>

589
00:39:09.481 --> 00:39:15.273
<i>Ending the duality of your ego
and simplifying your life.</i>

590
00:39:15.404 --> 00:39:18.488
<i>And that really appealed to Steve.</i>

591
00:39:18.657 --> 00:39:23.322
It's based on taking off
and creating something for yourself.

592
00:39:23.453 --> 00:39:27.447
You know, giving life to your own life
in whatever way you wish to do it.

593
00:39:28.917 --> 00:39:31.000
<i>At the time
he was starting Apple,</i>

594
00:39:31.128 --> 00:39:34.917
<i>Steve was very actively looking
for a mentor.</i>

595
00:39:39.428 --> 00:39:43.217
<i>Kobun Chino would
become Jobs's spiritual advisor.</i>

596
00:39:43.390 --> 00:39:47.725
<i>Kobun encouraged Jobs
not to retreat into a monastery,</i>

597
00:39:47.853 --> 00:39:51.563
<i>but instead to find Zen
in his life and work.</i>

598
00:39:51.773 --> 00:39:54.857
<i>But they would argue
over the path to enlightenment.</i>

599
00:40:08.123 --> 00:40:14.916
Steve always says, "Make me monk.
Please make me monk."

600
00:40:15.047 --> 00:40:18.336
I say, "Not until proof."

601
00:40:20.093 --> 00:40:26.055
When I was living in California,
23 years ago...

602
00:40:26.183 --> 00:40:27.970
Midnight...

603
00:40:28.101 --> 00:40:32.892
I answered the doorbell and there he is.

604
00:40:33.023 --> 00:40:36.061
18 years old, he was.

605
00:40:36.193 --> 00:40:39.436
And he wanted to see me.

606
00:40:40.489 --> 00:40:44.073
And I looked into his eyes, and...

607
00:40:44.201 --> 00:40:48.070
They looked terrible,
but he is not crazy.

608
00:40:48.205 --> 00:40:51.414
I must talk with him.

609
00:40:51.541 --> 00:40:57.037
I took him for a walk
through the downtown of Los Altos.

610
00:40:58.090 --> 00:41:00.207
All stores closed.

611
00:41:00.342 --> 00:41:06.304
One bar called The Teacup was open.

612
00:41:07.724 --> 00:41:10.842
We sat down at the counter.

613
00:41:12.479 --> 00:41:17.725
I had Irish coffee and he had juice.

614
00:41:18.986 --> 00:41:23.071
After sipping, he started to talk.

615
00:41:24.241 --> 00:41:29.453
He said, "I feel I'm enlightened."

616
00:41:29.579 --> 00:41:32.367
"I don't know what to do with this."

617
00:41:35.669 --> 00:41:41.131
That's wonderful.
That is very wonderful.

618
00:41:43.135 --> 00:41:45.798
I need proof of it.

619
00:41:50.058 --> 00:41:53.597
A week later he came back

620
00:41:53.895 --> 00:41:59.107
with a little metal sheet in his hand.

621
00:42:00.277 --> 00:42:03.941
Many things were going,
wires going around...

622
00:42:04.072 --> 00:42:06.689
I didn't know what it was.

623
00:42:07.325 --> 00:42:12.286
It was a chip of a personal computer.

624
00:42:14.041 --> 00:42:20.129
He said, "I designed it.
My friend Woz helped me."

625
00:42:21.298 --> 00:42:26.510
"This is called Lisa."

626
00:42:26.636 --> 00:42:28.593
"I named it Lisa."

627
00:42:29.639 --> 00:42:34.851
Which is the name of his daughter.

628
00:42:36.271 --> 00:42:40.436
That was the origin of Apple Computer.

629
00:42:42.360 --> 00:42:49.324
And I'm still not quite sure
that was a true proof or not.

630
00:42:49.451 --> 00:42:54.663
He's brilliant, but too smart, I think.

631
00:42:59.377 --> 00:43:03.917
When you broke the Lisa story,
why was that important?

632
00:43:04.049 --> 00:43:07.463
There was a computer called "Lisa."

633
00:43:07.594 --> 00:43:12.714
And everybody wondered
who the computer was named after.

634
00:43:14.559 --> 00:43:17.973
I didn't choose
to name the computer "Lisa."

635
00:43:18.980 --> 00:43:22.439
I was obviously curious
about why it was named "Lisa".

636
00:43:24.027 --> 00:43:26.861
Fair or unfair, I think that was,

637
00:43:26.988 --> 00:43:30.356
to me,
that was a germane part of the story.

638
00:43:38.917 --> 00:43:43.252
<i>With your mercury mouth</i>

639
00:43:43.380 --> 00:43:47.590
<i>In the missionary times</i>

640
00:43:47.717 --> 00:43:51.176
<i>And your eyes like smoke</i>

641
00:43:51.304 --> 00:43:53.967
<i>I was 17, sitting in the quad.</i>

642
00:43:54.099 --> 00:43:57.809
<i>Early spring,
warm and cold at the same time.</i>

643
00:43:57.978 --> 00:44:03.645
And I look over, and there's
this guy I have never seen.

644
00:44:03.775 --> 00:44:09.692
I've been there for three years.
I can't believe how gorgeous he is.

645
00:44:09.823 --> 00:44:16.161
<i>And he starts to walk out of the quad,
and I followed him</i>

646
00:44:16.288 --> 00:44:19.702
<i>cos I thought,
"I've got to introduce myself to him."</i>

647
00:44:20.834 --> 00:44:24.999
And I'm going, "What do I say?"
I had no idea what to say.

648
00:44:25.130 --> 00:44:28.339
<i>A few months later,
I was working on a film.</i>

649
00:44:28.466 --> 00:44:33.962
<i>We worked all night long,
and he walks up out of the dark.</i>

650
00:44:34.097 --> 00:44:38.637
He was confident and awkward.
He was a study in contrasts.

651
00:44:38.768 --> 00:44:44.810
And he had jeans on that drooped
because they had so many holes in them.

652
00:44:44.983 --> 00:44:48.272
And he was very intentional,
very intense.

653
00:44:48.403 --> 00:44:53.990
<i>And then he handed me a poem
by Bob Dylan.</i>

654
00:44:54.159 --> 00:44:56.446
<i>"Sad-Eyed Lady of the Lowlands."</i>

655
00:44:59.080 --> 00:45:02.790
<i>He would re-write Dylan's songs
to fit his life.</i>

656
00:45:05.086 --> 00:45:09.501
And then he... he just scanned the quad

657
00:45:09.633 --> 00:45:13.502
and the darkness
that went over his face...

658
00:45:14.763 --> 00:45:21.681
The edge, the worry,
the dissonance, was shocking to me.

659
00:45:21.811 --> 00:45:25.555
And I was young enough where I thought,
"Did I say something wrong?"

660
00:45:25.690 --> 00:45:28.353
But later I realized
that wasn't what it was.

661
00:45:28.485 --> 00:45:30.477
That was part of who he was.

662
00:45:30.612 --> 00:45:33.275
And I mean, that was one of the things
that I was attracted to,

663
00:45:33.406 --> 00:45:36.274
is that he had a lot going on
inside him.

664
00:45:39.746 --> 00:45:43.831
Steve was a romantic,
and he really loved Chrisann.

665
00:45:44.000 --> 00:45:47.334
<i>I think she was
a seductive force in his life,</i>

666
00:45:47.462 --> 00:45:54.005
<i>and there was a part of Steve
that didn't want to push that away.</i>

667
00:45:54.135 --> 00:45:56.422
But the main thing in Steve's life,
number one,

668
00:45:56.554 --> 00:46:00.093
was getting Apple off the ground.

669
00:46:00.225 --> 00:46:03.684
<i>And he just really could not focus
on anything else.</i>

670
00:46:05.230 --> 00:46:08.268
<i>I came out in June of '77,</i>

671
00:46:08.400 --> 00:46:13.520
<i>and the three of us went
and rented a house in Cupertino.</i>

672
00:46:15.282 --> 00:46:19.401
<i>Apple is beginning.
Steve and I are falling in love again.</i>

673
00:46:19.536 --> 00:46:22.153
But we're going back and forth
big time now.

674
00:46:22.289 --> 00:46:26.624
It's just like I'm insecure
because he's so unkind,

675
00:46:26.751 --> 00:46:30.165
and then we connect.

676
00:46:32.549 --> 00:46:36.042
<i>But I don't know how to handle
how fast Steve's mind is</i>

677
00:46:36.177 --> 00:46:39.295
<i>and how fast
he throws negative stuff at me.</i>

678
00:46:39.472 --> 00:46:44.934
And by the time I figure out,
"I've got to get out of here..."

679
00:46:46.229 --> 00:46:48.437
Um...

680
00:46:48.565 --> 00:46:51.478
"This is not working."

681
00:46:51.609 --> 00:46:57.571
Um... "I don't want to be in their club,
Daniel's or just even with Steve."

682
00:46:57.699 --> 00:47:03.320
"It's just not working."
That's when I got pregnant.

683
00:47:04.873 --> 00:47:07.866
What happened when
you told Steve that you were pregnant?

684
00:47:09.377 --> 00:47:10.788
Um...

685
00:47:10.920 --> 00:47:13.583
I told Steve in the dining room.

686
00:47:13.840 --> 00:47:18.926
Steve's jaw clenched.

687
00:47:19.179 --> 00:47:22.923
And searing anger...

688
00:47:23.099 --> 00:47:26.137
And he runs out the door,
kind of like a teenager,

689
00:47:26.269 --> 00:47:27.805
slams the door.

690
00:47:39.032 --> 00:47:41.274
<i>She got pregnant.</i>

691
00:47:41.409 --> 00:47:46.495
And Steve just was, "Not... not...
not me."

692
00:47:46.623 --> 00:47:49.161
"It's not me. It's not me," right?

693
00:47:49.334 --> 00:47:53.954
Even though that was not
a reasonable thing to say.

694
00:47:55.965 --> 00:47:59.458
<i>After Lisa was born,
Steve came up three days later.</i>

695
00:47:59.594 --> 00:48:02.302
<i>And we're sitting in a field,
and he...</i>

696
00:48:02.430 --> 00:48:05.423
We're like, trying to negotiate...

697
00:48:07.477 --> 00:48:10.811
...what name we both feel good about
for her.

698
00:48:10.939 --> 00:48:13.602
He knows he's the father.

699
00:48:15.193 --> 00:48:19.938
<i>He comes with the idea
of wanting to call her Claire,</i>

700
00:48:20.073 --> 00:48:23.783
<i>and I don't want Claire because
it's too much like Clara,</i>

701
00:48:23.910 --> 00:48:25.572
<i>his mother's name.</i>

702
00:48:25.703 --> 00:48:27.319
So, we're looking through the book,

703
00:48:27.455 --> 00:48:29.617
and we're thinking
and going back and forth,

704
00:48:29.749 --> 00:48:32.332
trying different names,
and finally I go, "Lisa!"

705
00:48:33.628 --> 00:48:37.963
<i>He said, "Yeah!"
We both loved that name.</i>

706
00:48:38.091 --> 00:48:44.759
<i>But later I realized he wanted
to name a line of computers</i>

707
00:48:44.889 --> 00:48:47.051
<i>or the next computer
the "Claire."</i>

708
00:48:47.225 --> 00:48:50.559
<i>I only knew this later.</i>

709
00:48:50.687 --> 00:48:55.899
He went back to Apple
and changed it to the "Lisa."

710
00:49:01.823 --> 00:49:04.861
<i>It says a lot about somebody</i>

711
00:49:04.993 --> 00:49:11.957
that they would have the wit,
the imagination, the audacity,

712
00:49:12.083 --> 00:49:16.999
to name a computer in the fashion
that Steve named this

713
00:49:17.130 --> 00:49:23.001
and believe that you're going to
be able to get away with it.

714
00:49:25.513 --> 00:49:29.507
<i>That is the sort
of very telling anecdote</i>

715
00:49:29.642 --> 00:49:33.477
<i>that helps illuminate
somebody's personality.</i>

716
00:49:41.529 --> 00:49:45.489
<i>My biological mother was
a young, unwed graduate student,</i>

717
00:49:45.617 --> 00:49:48.485
<i>and she decided
to put me up for adoption.</i>

718
00:49:48.620 --> 00:49:50.327
<i>So, everything was all set</i>

719
00:49:50.455 --> 00:49:53.948
<i>for me to be adopted at birth
by a lawyer and his wife.</i>

720
00:49:54.083 --> 00:49:56.075
Except that when I popped out,

721
00:49:56.252 --> 00:50:00.087
they decided at the last minute
that they really wanted a girl.

722
00:50:00.256 --> 00:50:02.669
So, my parents,
who were on a waiting list,

723
00:50:02.842 --> 00:50:05.755
got a call in the middle
of the night, asking,

724
00:50:05.970 --> 00:50:09.839
"We've got an unexpected baby boy.
Do you want him?"

725
00:50:09.974 --> 00:50:12.307
They said, "Of course."

726
00:50:13.311 --> 00:50:15.644
My biological mother found out later

727
00:50:15.772 --> 00:50:18.355
that my mother had never
graduated from college

728
00:50:18.525 --> 00:50:21.484
and that my father had never
graduated from high school.

729
00:50:21.653 --> 00:50:25.738
She refused to sign
the final adoption papers.

730
00:50:25.865 --> 00:50:27.731
She only relented a few months later

731
00:50:27.867 --> 00:50:31.827
when my parents promised
that I would go to college.

732
00:50:32.038 --> 00:50:34.826
This was the start in my life.

733
00:50:38.044 --> 00:50:41.788
<i>You know, another paradox
for him, you know, here's a guy,</i>

734
00:50:41.923 --> 00:50:44.666
<i>you know, being pissed off
that he was left for adoption,</i>

735
00:50:44.801 --> 00:50:47.635
<i>and when he has a child,
he wants to run the other way.</i>

736
00:50:47.762 --> 00:50:50.220
<i>Yes, that's a huge paradox.</i>

737
00:50:50.390 --> 00:50:55.602
Even when I first met Steve, the fact
that he was given up for adoption

738
00:50:55.728 --> 00:51:01.690
was a huge emotional issue
in his life.

739
00:51:01.818 --> 00:51:05.607
<i>I was, I remember,
right here on the lawn</i>

740
00:51:05.738 --> 00:51:09.948
<i>telling Lisa McMoyler, who lived
across the street, that I was adopted,</i>

741
00:51:10.076 --> 00:51:14.116
<i>and she said, "So, does that mean
your real parents didn't want you?"</i>

742
00:51:14.247 --> 00:51:17.115
<i>Ooh, lightening bolt. I remember
running into the house.</i>

743
00:51:17.250 --> 00:51:21.335
<i>I think I started crying, asking
my parents, and they sat me down.</i>

744
00:51:21.462 --> 00:51:23.374
<i>They said,
"No, you don't understand."</i>

745
00:51:23.506 --> 00:51:26.624
<i>They said,
"We specifically picked you."</i>

746
00:51:29.095 --> 00:51:33.339
<i>That was clearly
a very defining image in his life,</i>

747
00:51:33.516 --> 00:51:36.759
<i>both that he was rejected
and that he was special.</i>

748
00:51:39.689 --> 00:51:41.772
<i>The IPO was November 1980.</i>

749
00:51:41.899 --> 00:51:45.267
<i>By the summer of 1980,
it was clear it was going to happen,</i>

750
00:51:45.403 --> 00:51:48.487
<i>and so Steve's net worth
was going to go from $10 million</i>

751
00:51:48.615 --> 00:51:51.232
<i>to around $200 million.</i>

752
00:51:51.409 --> 00:51:56.655
<i>And I think he had the opportunity
to completely reinvent himself.</i>

753
00:51:57.874 --> 00:52:02.164
<i>In his reinvention, some
people who helped him were left behind.</i>

754
00:52:03.129 --> 00:52:04.961
<i>Woz had no taste for management,</i>

755
00:52:05.089 --> 00:52:09.254
<i>so he left Apple with a big stock
package and a lifetime stipend.</i>

756
00:52:10.178 --> 00:52:14.468
<i>Daniel Kottke had been
one of Apple's first employees.</i>

757
00:52:14.599 --> 00:52:17.467
<i>In the run-up to the IPO,
an Apple executive offered</i>

758
00:52:17.602 --> 00:52:20.561
<i>to give Daniel the same amount
of stock that Steve would give.</i>

759
00:52:20.688 --> 00:52:24.898
<i>Steve replied,
"Fine. I'll give him zero."</i>

760
00:52:29.072 --> 00:52:33.442
<i>Jobs also saw an opportunity
to rewrite his history with Chrisann.</i>

761
00:52:33.576 --> 00:52:38.196
<i>He composed a fiction which implied
she had many sexual partners,</i>

762
00:52:38.331 --> 00:52:40.288
<i>and he claimed he was sterile</i>

763
00:52:40.458 --> 00:52:45.169
<i>and therefore did not have the physical
capacity to procreate a child.</i>

764
00:52:48.675 --> 00:52:53.170
<i>A woman with a baby,
and I was that threatening to them.</i>

765
00:52:53.304 --> 00:52:56.638
<i>If he'd said, "I can't do this,
but let me help</i>

766
00:52:56.766 --> 00:52:59.884
because I can be practical here,"

767
00:53:00.019 --> 00:53:02.011
that would have been...

768
00:53:02.188 --> 00:53:04.771
...made for so much.

769
00:53:04.899 --> 00:53:09.109
But it almost seemed that the point
was to be out of integrity.

770
00:53:10.571 --> 00:53:14.281
<i>When a court-ordered DNA test
proved Jobs's paternity,</i>

771
00:53:14.450 --> 00:53:17.693
<i>he stopped fighting Chrisann
in court.</i>

772
00:53:17.870 --> 00:53:19.702
<i>She was on welfare at the time,</i>

773
00:53:19.872 --> 00:53:24.958
<i>so Jobs reluctantly agreed to pay
$500 a month in child support.</i>

774
00:53:26.462 --> 00:53:30.832
<i>When Apple went public,
he was worth nearly $200 million.</i>

775
00:53:30.967 --> 00:53:33.710
<i>Steve is so hugely successful,</i>

776
00:53:33.845 --> 00:53:37.509
<i>and yet he treated
so many people so badly.</i>

777
00:53:37.682 --> 00:53:41.926
How much of an asshole
do you have to be to be successful?

778
00:53:42.061 --> 00:53:44.018
What is the moral of the story here?

779
00:53:44.147 --> 00:53:46.389
<i>Hello. I am Macintosh.</i>

780
00:53:46.566 --> 00:53:50.276
<i>It is with considerable pride
that I introduce a man</i>

781
00:53:50.403 --> 00:53:53.692
<i>who's been like a father to me.
Steve Jobs.</i>

782
00:54:14.677 --> 00:54:17.545
<i>He didn't know
what real connection was.</i>

783
00:54:19.182 --> 00:54:24.268
So he was a part of the technology
that connected the world.

784
00:54:25.188 --> 00:54:27.054
Does that make sense?

785
00:54:29.025 --> 00:54:32.735
He made up another kind of connection.

786
00:54:39.035 --> 00:54:41.072
You know,
I didn't sleep a wink last night.

787
00:54:41.245 --> 00:54:47.412
There's a version of Steve Jobs
presenting the iPhone

788
00:54:47.585 --> 00:54:52.922
<i>where you can see his own feeling
of "I love this object."</i>

789
00:54:53.049 --> 00:54:54.631
Isn't this awesome?

790
00:54:54.842 --> 00:55:01.430
His stuff was beloved,
but it wasn't that he was beloved.

791
00:55:01.599 --> 00:55:03.807
<i>He wasn't a nice guy.</i>

792
00:55:03.935 --> 00:55:06.518
<i>First, he had a reputation
as a womanizer,</i>

793
00:55:06.646 --> 00:55:07.932
<i>and then he had a reputation</i>

794
00:55:08.064 --> 00:55:13.059
<i>as sort of not caring about anybody
and as being kind of a tough guy.</i>

795
00:55:14.153 --> 00:55:19.114
<i>People are not connected to him
because of his character.</i>

796
00:55:19.242 --> 00:55:22.076
That is not people's connection to him.

797
00:55:22.203 --> 00:55:25.947
In <i>Be Here Now</i> by Ram Dass,

798
00:55:26.082 --> 00:55:30.326
one of the memories I still have
after all these years

799
00:55:30.461 --> 00:55:34.751
<i>was when someone goes into
a state of enlightenment...</i>

800
00:55:36.259 --> 00:55:40.128
<i>...but they do it while
they're still attached to their ego...</i>

801
00:55:41.264 --> 00:55:46.100
<i>They call that, as I recall him
saying it, "the golden chain."</i>

802
00:55:49.063 --> 00:55:52.022
<i>And that's what I feel
happened to Steve.</i>

803
00:55:53.484 --> 00:55:59.606
<i>He went into magnificence
and into enlightenment, but he...</i>

804
00:56:01.242 --> 00:56:03.108
He just...

805
00:56:04.120 --> 00:56:05.531
He blew it.

806
00:56:08.457 --> 00:56:11.040
<i>Steve Jobs blew it?</i>

807
00:56:11.168 --> 00:56:14.707
<i>How many people in the world
believe that?</i>

808
00:56:14.839 --> 00:56:18.048
<i>He made products everyone loved.</i>

809
00:56:18.217 --> 00:56:22.803
<i>He was the computer era's
most successful entrepreneur.</i>

810
00:56:22.930 --> 00:56:25.843
<i>How could anyone think he blew it?</i>

811
00:56:27.685 --> 00:56:30.143
<i>The entrepreneur's a person
who wants to shake things up,</i>

812
00:56:30.271 --> 00:56:34.060
who wants to change things,
who sees a better way of doing that.

813
00:56:34.191 --> 00:56:37.559
But he or she tends to be
a royal pain in the neck.

814
00:56:37.737 --> 00:56:40.650
<i>Apple computer has sued
its co-founder and former chairman,</i>

815
00:56:40.781 --> 00:56:43.319
<i>Steven Jobs, to stop him
from starting a rival company.</i>

816
00:56:43.451 --> 00:56:48.037
Jobs quit Apple last week in a bitter
fight with his board and management.

817
00:56:48.164 --> 00:56:49.746
<i>So, Apple is reorganizing.</i>

818
00:56:49.874 --> 00:56:53.663
<i>John Sculley is taking control
from Steven Jobs.</i>

819
00:56:53.794 --> 00:56:56.662
<i>Tell us about your departure
from Apple.</i>

820
00:56:56.839 --> 00:57:01.049
Oh, it was very painful. I'm not even
sure I want to talk about it. Um...

821
00:57:04.430 --> 00:57:06.843
What can I say? I hired the wrong guy.

822
00:57:06.974 --> 00:57:09.432
- That was Sculley?
- Yeah.

823
00:57:09.560 --> 00:57:15.682
<i>And... he destroyed everything
I'd spent ten years working for.</i>

824
00:57:30.915 --> 00:57:35.205
<i>What did you do
after you left Apple in 1985?</i>

825
00:57:35.378 --> 00:57:39.167
I started two companies.

826
00:57:39.298 --> 00:57:41.164
One was started

827
00:57:41.300 --> 00:57:46.546
<i>by buying the computer-graphics
division of Lucasfilm.</i>

828
00:57:46.722 --> 00:57:49.465
<i>We christened it Pixar.</i>

829
00:57:49.600 --> 00:57:53.139
<i>Pixar was acquired by Disney.</i>

830
00:57:53.270 --> 00:57:56.559
<i>I'm on the board of directors
of the Walt Disney Company.</i>

831
00:57:57.775 --> 00:58:00.392
<i>And the other was called NeXT.</i>

832
00:58:02.947 --> 00:58:06.065
From having left
or been bounced from Apple,

833
00:58:06.200 --> 00:58:08.317
did he have a kind of a chip
on his shoulder?

834
00:58:08.452 --> 00:58:13.368
Was there some...
Was this Steve in the wilderness?

835
00:58:13.499 --> 00:58:15.456
I don't think he felt he was
in the wilderness at all.

836
00:58:15.584 --> 00:58:19.498
I think he felt he was on a path.
He was on a mission.

837
00:58:19.630 --> 00:58:21.587
Where are we going to?

838
00:58:21.757 --> 00:58:24.340
<i>In 1986, after he had left Apple</i>

839
00:58:24.468 --> 00:58:27.302
<i>and was in the process
of starting this new company,</i>

840
00:58:27.513 --> 00:58:30.631
<i>"Esquire" convinced him to
give a journalist a week of his time.</i>

841
00:58:30.808 --> 00:58:33.391
<i>So, I basically spent that week
with him,</i>

842
00:58:33.519 --> 00:58:36.853
<i>talked to him a lot, went to dinner,
sat in on meetings,</i>

843
00:58:36.981 --> 00:58:41.442
and got to see Jobs as he was
at that moment in his life.

844
00:58:41.569 --> 00:58:44.812
But, I mean, I agree that...
Let me back up a bit.

845
00:58:44.947 --> 00:58:46.483
So, somebody's got to say,

846
00:58:46.615 --> 00:58:49.733
"Here's what we can do,
and we can make it happen,

847
00:58:49.869 --> 00:58:53.158
and here's the level of thing
we can ship in 16 months."

848
00:58:53.289 --> 00:58:55.656
And what I hear him saying is,

849
00:58:55.833 --> 00:58:58.246
"Well, anything more than
a port of Mac author, forget it."

850
00:58:58.377 --> 00:59:00.164
And boy, that just makes me smoke.

851
00:59:00.296 --> 00:59:02.834
<i>If he was in a meeting
and somebody said,</i>

852
00:59:03.007 --> 00:59:07.297
"Here's a great idea," and put the idea
out there and he didn't like it,

853
00:59:07.428 --> 00:59:09.715
he'd just chop the person
into mincemeat.

854
00:59:09.889 --> 00:59:11.881
The problem I've got, though,
is one,

855
00:59:12.016 --> 00:59:14.975
will everybody believe
that the stake is in fact in the ground,

856
00:59:15.102 --> 00:59:16.809
and, secondly,
when software comes back

857
00:59:16.937 --> 00:59:19.054
and says what they can do
by summer or spring of '87,

858
00:59:19.190 --> 00:59:20.431
will they be telling us the truth?

859
00:59:20.608 --> 00:59:22.816
Well, George, I can't change the world,
you know.

860
00:59:22.943 --> 00:59:25.105
What do you want me to do?
What's the solution?

861
00:59:25.237 --> 00:59:26.899
But you see,
what we can learn is...

862
00:59:27.031 --> 00:59:29.318
What I want is probably irrelevant.

863
00:59:29.450 --> 00:59:31.567
I mean,
there are certain realities here,

864
00:59:31.702 --> 00:59:35.036
both psychological and market,

865
00:59:35.164 --> 00:59:39.124
that are going to come into play,
in my own personal judgment.

866
00:59:39.251 --> 00:59:42.119
And I think this is a window
that we've got. We've been given it.

867
00:59:42.254 --> 00:59:44.712
And thank God we've been given it.
Nobody else has done this.

868
00:59:44.882 --> 00:59:47.124
It's a wonderful window.
We have 18 months.

869
00:59:48.177 --> 00:59:52.046
<i>The article was about people
who are maniacal about work.</i>

870
00:59:52.181 --> 00:59:54.924
<i>And Steve Jobs was the most maniacal
person I could think of,</i>

871
00:59:55.142 --> 00:59:57.225
<i>which is why I wanted
to write about him.</i>

872
00:59:57.353 --> 01:00:00.061
<i>You made the connection
in that first piece a bunch of times,</i>

873
01:00:00.189 --> 01:00:01.976
<i>you know, the monk among priests.</i>

874
01:00:02.108 --> 01:00:05.226
<i>What was the relationship
between that extreme</i>

875
01:00:05.361 --> 01:00:08.274
<i>of working 24/7
and that monastic life?</i>

876
01:00:08.405 --> 01:00:12.240
I mean, he did seem to have
that rather interesting dedication.

877
01:00:12.368 --> 01:00:17.159
OK, so, a monomaniacal commitment
to something

878
01:00:17.289 --> 01:00:19.576
is something
that most people don't have.

879
01:00:20.918 --> 01:00:28.633
<i>And that, like the monk, requires you
to kind of shed extraneous things,</i>

880
01:00:28.759 --> 01:00:32.093
<i>and Steve Jobs absolutely,
positively had that.</i>

881
01:00:46.777 --> 01:00:48.894
<i>Jobs talked about
becoming a monk</i>

882
01:00:49.029 --> 01:00:53.069
<i>at this remote Zen temple
where Kobun Chino had studied.</i>

883
01:00:59.915 --> 01:01:02.407
<i>I wonder what he liked
about the idea of it.</i>

884
01:01:05.546 --> 01:01:10.587
<i>Was it the discipline of the monks?
Their unwavering focus?</i>

885
01:01:28.402 --> 01:01:31.691
<i>In meditation, Jobs loved
inspecting his own mind</i>

886
01:01:31.822 --> 01:01:33.779
<i>and changing the way it worked.</i>

887
01:01:33.949 --> 01:01:36.566
<i>He focused on the spirit of things</i>

888
01:01:36.702 --> 01:01:39.866
<i>and sought perfection
in the machines he made.</i>

889
01:01:40.748 --> 01:01:44.412
<i>But Kobun thought
Jobs was missing the point.</i>

890
01:01:44.585 --> 01:01:47.953
<i>A search for perfection
would never bring him peace</i>

891
01:01:48.088 --> 01:01:50.671
<i>or harmony with those around him.</i>

892
01:01:52.968 --> 01:01:56.837
<i>But maybe harmony is
what Jobs was looking for in Japan.</i>

893
01:01:58.682 --> 01:02:03.097
<i>He went there dozens of times,
and not just for business.</i>

894
01:02:03.229 --> 01:02:06.393
<i>He stayed in fancy hotels,
not Zen temples,</i>

895
01:02:06.523 --> 01:02:09.732
<i>but right to the end,
he kept going back.</i>

896
01:02:15.908 --> 01:02:18.651
<i>After he met and later married
Laurene Powell,</i>

897
01:02:18.786 --> 01:02:21.529
<i>he would take three
of his four children there,</i>

898
01:02:21.664 --> 01:02:23.496
<i>including Lisa.</i>

899
01:02:26.085 --> 01:02:29.249
<i>Jobs's relationship with Lisa
remained full of conflict,</i>

900
01:02:29.463 --> 01:02:34.174
<i>but a few years before Jobs's death,
Lisa wrote about a moment of peace.</i>

901
01:02:36.512 --> 01:02:40.677
<i>I didn't live with him, but he
would stop by our house some days,</i>

902
01:02:40.891 --> 01:02:44.601
<i>a deity among us for
a few tingly moments or hours.</i>

903
01:02:46.146 --> 01:02:49.264
<i>He was a more extreme vegetarian
than my mother and I</i>

904
01:02:49.400 --> 01:02:51.357
<i>and sharp-focused.</i>

905
01:02:51.485 --> 01:02:55.604
<i>One day, he spit out a mouthful of soup
after hearing it contained butter.</i>

906
01:02:55.739 --> 01:02:59.949
<i>With him, one ate a variety of salads.</i>

907
01:03:01.370 --> 01:03:05.159
<i>He believed that great harvests
came from arid sources.</i>

908
01:03:05.291 --> 01:03:07.328
<i>Pleasure from restraint.</i>

909
01:03:07.459 --> 01:03:11.669
<i>He knew the equations
that most people didn't know.</i>

910
01:03:11.797 --> 01:03:14.585
<i>Things led to their opposites.</i>

911
01:03:16.218 --> 01:03:19.757
<i>But once, he took me with him
on a business trip to Tokyo</i>

912
01:03:19.888 --> 01:03:23.973
<i>where we went to a sushi bar
in the basement of the Okura hotel</i>

913
01:03:24.101 --> 01:03:28.846
<i>with its high ceilings and low couches,
like a Hitchcock set.</i>

914
01:03:28.981 --> 01:03:33.271
<i>He ordered great trays of unagi sushi,
cooked eel on rice.</i>

915
01:03:33.402 --> 01:03:37.442
<i>He ordered too many pieces, knowing
we wouldn't be able to finish them,</i>

916
01:03:37.573 --> 01:03:40.941
<i>but that we didn't want
to feel they would run out.</i>

917
01:03:41.118 --> 01:03:44.111
<i>It was the first time I'd felt,
with him,</i>

918
01:03:44.246 --> 01:03:48.160
<i>so relaxed and content
over those trays of meat.</i>

919
01:03:48.292 --> 01:03:53.003
<i>The excess, the permission and warmth
after the cold salads</i>

920
01:03:53.172 --> 01:03:58.463
<i>meant a once inaccessible space
had opened.</i>

921
01:03:58.594 --> 01:04:01.302
<i>He was less rigid with himself,</i>

922
01:04:01.430 --> 01:04:05.174
<i>even human under the great ceilings
with the little chairs,</i>

923
01:04:05.392 --> 01:04:07.554
<i>with the meat and me.</i>

924
01:04:09.605 --> 01:04:14.600
<i>But the event was not self-sustaining.
We went back home to salads.</i>

925
01:04:14.735 --> 01:04:18.695
<i>They satisfied me less
now that I knew the alternative.</i>

926
01:04:24.244 --> 01:04:27.863
- What eventually happened to NeXT?
- Apple purchased it.

927
01:04:27.998 --> 01:04:33.289
- OK, when?
- I believe 1997.

928
01:04:33.462 --> 01:04:37.923
<i>When Apple bought NeXT,
Apple was pretty messed up.</i>

929
01:04:38.050 --> 01:04:39.791
<i>It was pretty easy to see.</i>

930
01:04:39.927 --> 01:04:41.043
Apple Computer,

931
01:04:41.178 --> 01:04:43.465
a pioneer in the personal computers
and software business,

932
01:04:43.597 --> 01:04:44.883
has fallen on hard times.

933
01:04:45.015 --> 01:04:46.347
<i>Over a three-month period,</i>

934
01:04:46.475 --> 01:04:50.435
<i>Apple's profits plunged
by more than $50 million.</i>

935
01:04:50.562 --> 01:04:54.397
<i>With big losses in the last
quarter, with profit margins shrinking,</i>

936
01:04:54.525 --> 01:04:56.642
<i>Apple seems destined for a takeover.</i>

937
01:04:56.777 --> 01:04:59.645
<i>Steve Jobs co-founded Apple
with Steve Wozniak,</i>

938
01:04:59.780 --> 01:05:02.363
<i>and on Friday,
Apple went to the well once again,</i>

939
01:05:02.491 --> 01:05:04.232
<i>bringing Jobs back as a consultant,</i>

940
01:05:04.368 --> 01:05:06.655
<i>writing one
of the most unlikely chapters ever</i>

941
01:05:06.870 --> 01:05:08.486
<i>in the lore that is Silicon Valley.</i>

942
01:05:08.622 --> 01:05:09.954
Steve.

943
01:05:20.467 --> 01:05:23.960
<i>I joined the company
in February '97.</i>

944
01:05:24.096 --> 01:05:27.464
After a couple of days there,
I was in complete shock.

945
01:05:29.810 --> 01:05:33.520
The company was close to bankruptcy,
and it was total chaos.

946
01:05:33.647 --> 01:05:36.811
<i>The NeXT acquisition
had just occurred,</i>

947
01:05:36.942 --> 01:05:39.025
<i>and there were major changes
going on.</i>

948
01:05:39.236 --> 01:05:43.651
<i>And Steve was involved at that point
in time, but on the margins.</i>

949
01:05:43.782 --> 01:05:46.570
I haven't been back here
in over ten years,

950
01:05:46.702 --> 01:05:50.446
so, yeah, it's an interesting feeling.

951
01:05:50.622 --> 01:05:53.239
It's a little strange,
but not too strange.

952
01:05:53.375 --> 01:05:54.832
<i>At some point in time,</i>

953
01:05:54.960 --> 01:05:57.953
<i>the process was started
to look for a full-time CEO.</i>

954
01:05:58.088 --> 01:06:00.330
<i>And at that point in time,
Steve got much more involved.</i>

955
01:06:00.466 --> 01:06:01.752
<i>Now, he still wasn't the CEO.</i>

956
01:06:01.884 --> 01:06:05.343
I don't think he was 100% sure
the company was savable yet,

957
01:06:05.471 --> 01:06:08.054
and so I think he was
hedging his bets a little bit.

958
01:06:08.223 --> 01:06:12.843
<i>He had to make a decision whether he
really wanted to take on that role</i>

959
01:06:12.978 --> 01:06:16.312
<i>because being CEO of Apple
is an all-consuming role,</i>

960
01:06:16.440 --> 01:06:18.932
<i>and I'm not sure Steve thought
that that's something</i>

961
01:06:19.067 --> 01:06:23.232
<i>that he wanted to do for what
turned out to be the rest of his life.</i>

962
01:06:23.363 --> 01:06:28.904
I took the title of Interim CEO
and agreed to come back for 90 days

963
01:06:29.036 --> 01:06:32.746
to help recruit a full-time CEO.

964
01:06:35.959 --> 01:06:38.246
How did that recruitment effort go?

965
01:06:38.378 --> 01:06:40.085
I failed.

966
01:06:45.928 --> 01:06:48.716
<i>You know, the real hero
of that early part of the story</i>

967
01:06:48.847 --> 01:06:50.088
<i>is Fred Anderson.</i>

968
01:06:50.265 --> 01:06:53.508
<i>Fred restructured the company
financially</i>

969
01:06:53.644 --> 01:06:56.762
and bought us the time to build
the foundation for today's Apple.

970
01:06:56.897 --> 01:07:00.015
<i>And then Fred even had an impact
on the product strategy</i>

971
01:07:00.150 --> 01:07:02.642
<i>because when Steve came back,</i>

972
01:07:02.778 --> 01:07:06.567
the first major project we
started was a network computer.

973
01:07:06.740 --> 01:07:09.733
That was kind of the rage at the time,
but it wasn't a consumer product at all.

974
01:07:09.910 --> 01:07:11.572
<i>Fred kept going,
"You know, wait a minute,</i>

975
01:07:11.703 --> 01:07:13.285
<i>we got to have a consumer product."</i>

976
01:07:13.413 --> 01:07:15.120
<i>"You guys have to focus
on a low-end Mac</i>

977
01:07:15.290 --> 01:07:17.498
because that's what's going to
turn the company around."

978
01:07:18.293 --> 01:07:21.627
<i>So, the executive team and Steve
decided that we would switch</i>

979
01:07:21.755 --> 01:07:26.420
<i>from doing the network computer
and make that the iMac.</i>

980
01:07:26.635 --> 01:07:29.423
There you go, right here.
Can you see it?

981
01:07:31.515 --> 01:07:33.973
<i>She comes in colors everywhere</i>

982
01:07:34.101 --> 01:07:37.435
In the world of computers,
it's kill or be killed.

983
01:07:37.563 --> 01:07:40.397
And the original whiz kid was thought
to be dying an early death.

984
01:07:40.524 --> 01:07:44.143
We went for colors that really expressed
the spirit of the machine.

985
01:07:44.361 --> 01:07:47.229
And that is... you know,
it's powerful, but fun.

986
01:07:49.866 --> 01:07:52.483
<i>And the first thing
I thought I'd do is give you an update.</i>

987
01:07:52.703 --> 01:07:56.117
<i>We've managed to go from losing
a billion dollars the year before</i>

988
01:07:56.248 --> 01:08:01.243
<i>to actually making over $200 million
during the first three quarters.</i>

989
01:08:01.378 --> 01:08:03.335
Boy, what a difference a year makes.

990
01:08:03.463 --> 01:08:05.705
Guess what? Mac is back.

991
01:08:08.051 --> 01:08:11.385
<i>She comes in colors everywhere</i>

992
01:08:11.513 --> 01:08:13.505
<i>She combs her hair</i>

993
01:08:13.640 --> 01:08:15.882
<i>She's like a rainbow</i>

994
01:08:16.018 --> 01:08:19.978
He was the kind of person that
could convince himself of things

995
01:08:20.105 --> 01:08:22.597
that weren't necessarily true.

996
01:08:22.733 --> 01:08:26.226
<i>He could go to people
and ask them to do something</i>

997
01:08:26.403 --> 01:08:28.486
<i>that they thought was impossible.</i>

998
01:08:29.823 --> 01:08:32.736
<i>Steve did create
reality distortion around him.</i>

999
01:08:32.868 --> 01:08:35.030
You know, if he told you
the sky was green, for a while,

1000
01:08:35.162 --> 01:08:37.154
you'd kind of go, "Yeah, OK.
Yeah, the sky's green."

1001
01:08:39.875 --> 01:08:45.291
To me... marketing is about values.

1002
01:08:45.464 --> 01:08:50.380
This is a very complicated world.
It's a very noisy world.

1003
01:08:50.510 --> 01:08:53.799
And we're not going to get a chance

1004
01:08:53.930 --> 01:08:59.426
to get people to remember much
about us. No company is.

1005
01:09:00.479 --> 01:09:02.596
And so, we have to be really clear

1006
01:09:02.731 --> 01:09:05.064
on what we want them to know
about us.

1007
01:09:05.192 --> 01:09:07.400
Our customers want to know
who is Apple

1008
01:09:07.527 --> 01:09:09.393
and what is it that we stand for.

1009
01:09:09.529 --> 01:09:15.321
What we have is something
that I am...

1010
01:09:15.452 --> 01:09:17.910
...I am very moved by.

1011
01:09:21.208 --> 01:09:22.824
<i>Here's to the crazy ones.</i>

1012
01:09:22.959 --> 01:09:25.292
<i>Jobs was so moved
by the ad he'd commissioned</i>

1013
01:09:25.504 --> 01:09:28.997
<i>that he produced a version where
he did the voiceover himself.</i>

1014
01:09:29.132 --> 01:09:32.466
<i>The round pegs in the square holes.</i>

1015
01:09:32.594 --> 01:09:35.883
<i>The ones
who see things differently.</i>

1016
01:09:36.014 --> 01:09:40.475
<i>They're not fond of rules, and they
have no respect for the status quo.</i>

1017
01:09:41.687 --> 01:09:47.604
<i>You can quote them, disagree with them,
glorify or vilify them.</i>

1018
01:09:47.734 --> 01:09:51.068
<i>About the only thing
you can't do is ignore them</i>

1019
01:09:51.196 --> 01:09:53.108
<i>because they change things.</i>

1020
01:09:53.240 --> 01:09:59.362
<i>While some may see them
as the crazy ones, we see genius.</i>

1021
01:10:00.580 --> 01:10:04.199
<i>Because the people who are crazy enough
to think they can change the world...</i>

1022
01:10:05.460 --> 01:10:07.827
<i>...are the ones who do.</i>

1023
01:10:11.967 --> 01:10:15.711
It honors those people
who have changed the world.

1024
01:10:16.805 --> 01:10:21.266
Some of them are living,
some of them are not.

1025
01:10:21.393 --> 01:10:26.934
But the ones that aren't, you know
that if they ever used a computer,

1026
01:10:27.065 --> 01:10:28.226
it would have been a Mac.

1027
01:10:32.738 --> 01:10:34.855
The theme of the campaign is
"Think different."

1028
01:10:37.492 --> 01:10:39.154
<i>Think different.</i>

1029
01:10:39.286 --> 01:10:41.824
<i>In one brilliant, ungrammatical phrase,</i>

1030
01:10:41.955 --> 01:10:47.872
<i>Jobs told a story of rebellion,
the triumph of the iconoclastic genius.</i>

1031
01:10:48.003 --> 01:10:51.838
<i>With "Think different," was Jobs
trying to frame his own story?</i>

1032
01:10:52.007 --> 01:10:55.421
<i>More than a CEO,
he positioned himself as an oracle,</i>

1033
01:10:55.552 --> 01:10:58.886
<i>a man who could tell the future
of technology.</i>

1034
01:11:09.107 --> 01:11:10.848
<i>You know, a lot of times,</i>

1035
01:11:10.984 --> 01:11:16.150
<i>great products are sort of convergence
of the right set of technologies.</i>

1036
01:11:16.281 --> 01:11:19.695
<i>And Steve was brilliant
at getting to a fork in the road</i>

1037
01:11:19.826 --> 01:11:22.443
<i>and choosing the right fork.</i>

1038
01:11:22.621 --> 01:11:26.331
We got a chance to play
with a variety of music players,

1039
01:11:26.458 --> 01:11:28.245
and they sucked.

1040
01:11:28.418 --> 01:11:32.662
<i>So, we decided, Steve said,
you know, "Go build a music player."</i>

1041
01:11:32.798 --> 01:11:37.042
So, I assembled a small team to take
a look at what it would take to do it,

1042
01:11:37.177 --> 01:11:40.636
and the conclusion was the technology
really wasn't ready yet.

1043
01:11:40.764 --> 01:11:44.178
<i>Then in February of 2001,</i>

1044
01:11:44.309 --> 01:11:47.518
<i>the Toshiba guys brought out
the 1.8-inch hard drive.</i>

1045
01:11:47.646 --> 01:11:48.932
So, as soon as I saw that I go,

1046
01:11:49.064 --> 01:11:51.147
"That's what we need
to build the iPod."

1047
01:11:51.274 --> 01:11:55.268
<i>So, I went to Steve, and I go,
"OK, I know how to do it now."</i>

1048
01:11:55.403 --> 01:11:56.814
<i>"I need $10 million."</i>

1049
01:11:56.947 --> 01:12:00.440
<i>And Steve goes, "OK,
I'll write you a $10 million check."</i>

1050
01:12:00.617 --> 01:12:03.655
I went to Fred to make sure
the check wouldn't bounce,

1051
01:12:03.787 --> 01:12:06.279
and Fred said, "Yeah, you know, go."

1052
01:12:06.414 --> 01:12:08.576
<i>And so I started ramping the team up,</i>

1053
01:12:08.708 --> 01:12:12.577
<i>and, you know, we delivered
the iPod later that year.</i>

1054
01:12:14.881 --> 01:12:16.964
<i>One, two, three, four</i>

1055
01:12:17.092 --> 01:12:19.049
<i>Tell me that you love me more</i>

1056
01:12:19.177 --> 01:12:21.294
<i>Sleepless long nights</i>

1057
01:12:21.429 --> 01:12:23.386
<i>That is what my youth was for</i>

1058
01:12:23.515 --> 01:12:25.472
<i>Oh, oh, oh</i>

1059
01:12:25.642 --> 01:12:27.975
<i>You're changing your heart</i>

1060
01:12:28.103 --> 01:12:29.810
<i>Oh, oh, oh</i>

1061
01:12:29.980 --> 01:12:32.188
<i>You know who you are</i>

1062
01:12:32.315 --> 01:12:34.398
<i>One, two, three, four</i>

1063
01:12:34.526 --> 01:12:36.233
<i>Tell me that you love me more</i>

1064
01:12:36.361 --> 01:12:38.523
<i>Sleepless long nights</i>

1065
01:12:38.697 --> 01:12:40.609
<i>That is what my youth was for</i>

1066
01:12:40.740 --> 01:12:43.198
<i>Oh, teenage hopes</i>

1067
01:12:46.079 --> 01:12:49.948
<i>Jobs's genius
was how he sold the iPod.</i>

1068
01:12:50.083 --> 01:12:53.747
<i>It wasn't a machine for you.
It was you.</i>

1069
01:12:56.131 --> 01:12:59.295
<i>People sometimes forget
that they're very unique</i>

1070
01:12:59.426 --> 01:13:03.170
<i>and that they have very unique
feelings and perspectives.</i>

1071
01:13:03.388 --> 01:13:04.799
You know,
the whole computer industry

1072
01:13:04.931 --> 01:13:09.301
wants to forget about the humanist side
and just focus on the technology,

1073
01:13:09.436 --> 01:13:12.349
but we think there's
a whole other side to the coin,

1074
01:13:12.480 --> 01:13:14.563
which is what do you do
with these things?

1075
01:13:14.733 --> 01:13:18.693
Can we do more than just spreadsheets
and word processors?

1076
01:13:18.820 --> 01:13:21.813
Can we help you express yourself
in richer ways?

1077
01:13:24.743 --> 01:13:29.078
<i>Apple at the core, its core value,</i>

1078
01:13:29.205 --> 01:13:33.575
<i>is that we believe
that people with passion</i>

1079
01:13:33.752 --> 01:13:35.869
<i>can change the world for the better.</i>

1080
01:13:36.004 --> 01:13:37.961
That's what we believe.

1081
01:13:39.674 --> 01:13:42.257
<i>Steve talks a lot
about the values of the company.</i>

1082
01:13:42.385 --> 01:13:44.217
<i>And said that Apple was a company</i>

1083
01:13:44.346 --> 01:13:47.259
<i>that was designed
to make the world a better place.</i>

1084
01:13:47.390 --> 01:13:50.053
Was that a heartfelt thing for Steve?

1085
01:13:50.185 --> 01:13:52.552
I believe it was
a heartfelt thing for Steve.

1086
01:13:52.729 --> 01:13:55.312
I think that he did want to make
the world a better place.

1087
01:13:55.523 --> 01:14:00.063
I think that he felt
by delivering great products

1088
01:14:00.195 --> 01:14:03.814
that were easy to use and beautiful,

1089
01:14:03.949 --> 01:14:06.407
that it would
make people's lives better.

1090
01:14:09.788 --> 01:14:11.370
<i>Is that enough?</i>

1091
01:14:11.498 --> 01:14:15.117
<i>Is making and selling products,
even if they're good,</i>

1092
01:14:15.251 --> 01:14:18.870
<i>even if they're the best, enough
to make the world a better place?</i>

1093
01:14:20.799 --> 01:14:22.210
<i>Apple's a business.</i>

1094
01:14:22.342 --> 01:14:25.506
And we've somehow attached
this emotion to a business,

1095
01:14:25.720 --> 01:14:29.339
which is just there to make money
for its shareholders, right?

1096
01:14:29.474 --> 01:14:30.931
That's all it is. Nothing more.

1097
01:14:31.059 --> 01:14:32.891
<i>You know, creating that association</i>

1098
01:14:33.103 --> 01:14:36.517
<i>was probably one of Steve's
greatest accomplishments.</i>

1099
01:14:36.648 --> 01:14:39.015
- It's queued up to play.
- Awesome.

1100
01:14:41.945 --> 01:14:45.780
I remember at this point, when the music
plays in the beginning,

1101
01:14:45.907 --> 01:14:47.569
there's just this energy, right?

1102
01:14:47.742 --> 01:14:52.533
You have on the one side
this huge bank of photographers,

1103
01:14:52.664 --> 01:14:56.578
<i>and I remember
looking at all these guys</i>

1104
01:14:56.751 --> 01:14:59.243
with their cameras trained on Steve,
thinking,

1105
01:14:59.379 --> 01:15:02.122
"You guys have no idea
what's about to happen."

1106
01:15:02.257 --> 01:15:03.964
And to be fair, neither did we.

1107
01:15:07.262 --> 01:15:09.595
Thank you for coming.

1108
01:15:09.764 --> 01:15:11.926
We're going to make some history
together today.

1109
01:15:13.059 --> 01:15:14.766
<i>Any time
you see an Apple event,</i>

1110
01:15:14.894 --> 01:15:18.934
know that there's a team of people
in the audience who are just sick.

1111
01:15:22.861 --> 01:15:26.400
We are calling it "iPhone."

1112
01:15:28.199 --> 01:15:34.821
Today... Today,
Apple is going to reinvent the phone.

1113
01:15:34.956 --> 01:15:38.700
And so, rather than talk about this
some more, let me show it to you.

1114
01:15:38.877 --> 01:15:42.917
<i>So, if you're giving a demo,
and you deviate off the script,</i>

1115
01:15:43.048 --> 01:15:44.505
<i>well, lots of bad things
can go wrong.</i>

1116
01:15:44.632 --> 01:15:48.376
When Steve comes up with,
"Here's what I want to show,"

1117
01:15:48.511 --> 01:15:50.002
everything is dissected.

1118
01:15:50.180 --> 01:15:51.512
The message that he's trying to say

1119
01:15:51.639 --> 01:15:53.596
is then dissected
into very specific actions.

1120
01:15:53.725 --> 01:15:57.719
And let me go ahead and get
that picture within picture up.

1121
01:15:57.896 --> 01:16:01.060
I'm going to go ahead and just push
the "sleep wake" button.

1122
01:16:01.191 --> 01:16:03.103
There we go, right there.

1123
01:16:03.234 --> 01:16:06.568
And to unlock the phone, I just take
my finger and slide it across.

1124
01:16:06.696 --> 01:16:09.154
All right, you want to see that again?

1125
01:16:10.825 --> 01:16:14.034
<i>So, he's got, you know,
several discrete parts of the demo.</i>

1126
01:16:14.245 --> 01:16:20.037
We had a flask of Scotch with us,
and after every little part,

1127
01:16:20.168 --> 01:16:24.003
the person who was responsible for
that portion, you know, took a hit.

1128
01:16:24.130 --> 01:16:26.497
I want to make a call to Jony Ive.

1129
01:16:26.716 --> 01:16:27.923
I can just push here,

1130
01:16:28.051 --> 01:16:30.589
and I see Jony Ive's contacts
with all his information.

1131
01:16:30.720 --> 01:16:33.428
The Jony Ive call, oh, my God.

1132
01:16:33.556 --> 01:16:36.469
<i>There's all sorts of ways that
this could have gone sideways.</i>

1133
01:16:36.601 --> 01:16:39.935
- Hey, Jony, how you doing?
<i>- I'm good. How you doing?</i>

1134
01:16:40.063 --> 01:16:44.649
Well, it's been two-and-a-half years,
and I can't tell you how thrilled I am

1135
01:16:44.776 --> 01:16:47.564
to make the first public phone call
with iPhone.

1136
01:16:50.532 --> 01:16:51.943
He goes to the music.

1137
01:16:52.075 --> 01:16:54.283
Let's go into Dylan here.

1138
01:16:54.410 --> 01:16:56.322
Let's play <i>Like a Rolling Stone.</i>

1139
01:16:58.998 --> 01:17:00.614
He gets the web browser up there.

1140
01:17:00.750 --> 01:17:04.243
I want to show you Safari
running on a mobile device.

1141
01:17:04.379 --> 01:17:06.541
So, let's go to the web.
Boom.

1142
01:17:06.673 --> 01:17:08.335
Unbelievable.

1143
01:17:08.466 --> 01:17:11.129
And then at the end,
he has that moment

1144
01:17:11.261 --> 01:17:13.298
where he swizzles it all together.

1145
01:17:13.429 --> 01:17:17.013
<i>At the end, where he orders thousands
of lattes from some, you know,</i>

1146
01:17:17.142 --> 01:17:19.429
<i>poor woman at a Starbucks
down the road.</i>

1147
01:17:19.561 --> 01:17:21.974
<i>Good morning. This is Starbucks
and how can I help you?</i>

1148
01:17:22.105 --> 01:17:24.563
Yes, I'd liked to order
4,000 lattes to go, please.

1149
01:17:24.691 --> 01:17:26.102
No, just kidding.

1150
01:17:26.234 --> 01:17:29.648
Wrong number. Thank you. Bye-bye.

1151
01:17:29.779 --> 01:17:31.486
OK.

1152
01:17:38.746 --> 01:17:41.284
As soon as the demo was over,
we left.

1153
01:17:41.416 --> 01:17:46.252
And we just turned San Francisco
into a... It was a shit show.

1154
01:17:53.595 --> 01:17:55.507
That was a night to remember.

1155
01:17:56.681 --> 01:18:00.220
<i>Man, you just had this release
of years of anxiety.</i>

1156
01:18:00.351 --> 01:18:03.594
And then we got up tomorrow,
the next day, and did it all over again.

1157
01:18:03.730 --> 01:18:05.596
And we had to finish the product
at this point.

1158
01:18:05.773 --> 01:18:08.311
And that was tough,
especially with a raging hangover,

1159
01:18:08.443 --> 01:18:10.355
but it was a lot of fun.

1160
01:18:10.486 --> 01:18:14.321
The biggest thing he made was
the iPhone, definitely.

1161
01:18:14.449 --> 01:18:15.985
He made the iPhone,

1162
01:18:16.159 --> 01:18:19.778
which shocked the world
with its touch screen and stuff.

1163
01:18:24.459 --> 01:18:28.829
So, what are we down to?
13 minutes and...

1164
01:18:29.005 --> 01:18:30.416
Whoo-hoo!

1165
01:18:30.548 --> 01:18:33.086
- It's go time!
- Oh, yeah.

1166
01:18:42.435 --> 01:18:46.475
- Oh, yeah. Swipe to unlock.
- Sweet!

1167
01:18:50.193 --> 01:18:53.686
<i>All my life</i>

1168
01:18:53.821 --> 01:18:56.063
<i>Is changing every day</i>

1169
01:18:56.199 --> 01:18:57.690
There she is.

1170
01:18:57.909 --> 01:19:00.071
<i>In every possible way</i>

1171
01:19:00.203 --> 01:19:03.696
iPhone's been shipping
for exactly 200 days today.

1172
01:19:03.831 --> 01:19:07.040
And I'm extraordinarily pleased
to report

1173
01:19:07.168 --> 01:19:10.502
that we have sold
four million iPhones to date.

1174
01:19:12.465 --> 01:19:15.503
<i>Never quite as it seems</i>

1175
01:19:15.635 --> 01:19:19.003
<i>And then I open up and see</i>

1176
01:19:19.138 --> 01:19:23.553
<i>The person falling here is me</i>

1177
01:19:23.685 --> 01:19:27.645
Today we're introducing
the iPhone 3G.

1178
01:19:33.653 --> 01:19:39.194
In a little over two years,
we have sold 30 million iPhones.

1179
01:19:47.542 --> 01:19:50.250
And with a swipe,
you have changed your life.

1180
01:19:50.378 --> 01:19:52.210
Yes!

1181
01:20:04.517 --> 01:20:08.431
- How may I help you?
- iPhone 4. Where is the iPhone 4?

1182
01:20:08.563 --> 01:20:12.398
Oh, I'm very sorry,
but we are currently sold out.

1183
01:20:12.525 --> 01:20:16.690
However, we did finally get
some more HTC Evos in.

1184
01:20:16.821 --> 01:20:19.780
What? What is that?
Is it an iPhone?

1185
01:20:19.907 --> 01:20:22.695
No, it is that 4G phone on Sprint.

1186
01:20:22.827 --> 01:20:25.240
If it's not an iPhone,
why would I want it?

1187
01:20:25.371 --> 01:20:28.364
Well, it's similar to an iPhone,
but has a bigger screen.

1188
01:20:28.499 --> 01:20:29.660
I don't care.

1189
01:20:29.792 --> 01:20:32.455
The internet speeds
are around three times faster.

1190
01:20:32.587 --> 01:20:35.204
- I don't care.
- It fucking prints money.

1191
01:20:35.340 --> 01:20:38.253
- I don't care.
- It can grant up to three wishes.

1192
01:20:38.384 --> 01:20:40.842
Even if one of those wishes
is for an iPhone.

1193
01:20:40.970 --> 01:20:43.462
- I don't care about any of that.
- OK, fine.

1194
01:20:43.598 --> 01:20:46.466
Then what the hell entices you
about the iPhone 4,

1195
01:20:46.601 --> 01:20:48.558
if you don't mind me asking?

1196
01:20:48.686 --> 01:20:50.393
It is an iPhone.

1197
01:21:00.615 --> 01:21:05.201
<i>I remember the first set of
people I interviewed about the iPhone.</i>

1198
01:21:05.328 --> 01:21:07.911
<i>I've been interviewing people
about their computers</i>

1199
01:21:08.039 --> 01:21:09.996
<i>for, you know, decades.</i>

1200
01:21:10.166 --> 01:21:16.333
<i>I've never seen this kind of connection
before with an object.</i>

1201
01:21:18.299 --> 01:21:21.918
<i>In the beginning,
the impulse was to sit you down</i>

1202
01:21:22.053 --> 01:21:25.763
<i>and to show you everything
on their iPhone.</i>

1203
01:21:25.973 --> 01:21:29.557
<i>As time's gone on,
there's been less of that</i>

1204
01:21:29.685 --> 01:21:34.350
<i>and more of what I call
the "alone together phenomenon."</i>

1205
01:21:37.860 --> 01:21:42.025
It has turned out to be
an isolating technology.

1206
01:21:53.376 --> 01:21:55.413
<i>Did you ever see
that Wim Wenders film,</i>

1207
01:21:55.545 --> 01:21:56.706
<i>"Until the End of the World"?</i>

1208
01:21:56.838 --> 01:22:00.832
<i>Yes, I love that.
People fall in love with their dreams,</i>

1209
01:22:00.967 --> 01:22:04.631
<i>and they walk around
with hoods over their head</i>

1210
01:22:04.762 --> 01:22:09.052
<i>and screens in front of them,
fascinated by their dreams.</i>

1211
01:22:09.225 --> 01:22:13.344
<i>By the time I came to rescue
Claire, the only thing she cared about</i>

1212
01:22:13.479 --> 01:22:16.847
<i>was having fresh batteries
for her video monitor.</i>

1213
01:22:19.068 --> 01:22:20.730
<i>It's a little bit like that.</i>

1214
01:22:20.862 --> 01:22:24.947
<i>It's a dream machine,
and you become fascinated</i>

1215
01:22:25.074 --> 01:22:28.988
<i>by the world that you can find
on these screens.</i>

1216
01:22:30.371 --> 01:22:34.706
<i>And the face of that technology
was Steve Jobs.</i>

1217
01:22:55.980 --> 01:23:00.020
What would you say about
the responsibilities of power

1218
01:23:00.151 --> 01:23:03.485
once you've achieved
a certain level of success?

1219
01:23:03.613 --> 01:23:07.027
Power? What is that?

1220
01:23:13.873 --> 01:23:16.331
<i>Your daddy, he's an outlaw</i>

1221
01:23:16.542 --> 01:23:18.909
<i>He found a loophole
where if you lease a car,</i>

1222
01:23:19.045 --> 01:23:22.834
you have a six-month grace period
to put license plates on.

1223
01:23:22.965 --> 01:23:28.552
And so he leased the same car
every six months,

1224
01:23:28.679 --> 01:23:30.841
to avoid putting license plates
at all.

1225
01:23:31.015 --> 01:23:32.677
<i>I think he told people</i>

1226
01:23:32.808 --> 01:23:37.394
that it's because he didn't
want people to identify him.

1227
01:23:37.522 --> 01:23:39.434
Well, there's nothing more identifiable

1228
01:23:39.565 --> 01:23:41.898
than a silver Mercedes
with no license plate.

1229
01:23:42.026 --> 01:23:44.643
I mean, it screams "Steve Jobs"
in the Valley.

1230
01:23:44.779 --> 01:23:48.568
Riding to work with Steve Jobs.

1231
01:23:48.699 --> 01:23:51.612
Riding to work
with the good old Stevie.

1232
01:23:53.955 --> 01:23:57.574
Oh, look at that,
he's in the carpool lane.

1233
01:23:57.708 --> 01:24:01.577
And it does give you a glimpse
of how he thought he was above the law.

1234
01:24:01.712 --> 01:24:06.332
<i>He oversees his kingdom
Where no stranger does intrude</i>

1235
01:24:06.509 --> 01:24:09.343
<i>His voice
It trembles as he calls out</i>

1236
01:24:09.470 --> 01:24:12.008
<i>For another plate of food</i>

1237
01:24:12.139 --> 01:24:15.598
<i>One more cup of coffee for the road</i>

1238
01:24:15.726 --> 01:24:19.436
<i>Jobs also made it a habit
to park his plateless Mercedes Benz</i>

1239
01:24:19.564 --> 01:24:22.477
<i>in handicap parking spots
around the Apple campus.</i>

1240
01:24:23.901 --> 01:24:26.393
<i>It even became something
of a pastime in the Valley,</i>

1241
01:24:26.529 --> 01:24:29.317
<i>to take a picture next to Steve's car.</i>

1242
01:24:30.616 --> 01:24:34.701
<i>One more cup of coffee before I go</i>

1243
01:24:34.829 --> 01:24:37.321
<i>To the valley below</i>

1244
01:24:41.627 --> 01:24:45.371
<i>He was a hero in the Valley
because he made buckets of money,</i>

1245
01:24:45.506 --> 01:24:46.917
<i>but unlike Bill Gates,</i>

1246
01:24:47.049 --> 01:24:50.838
<i>Jobs told people that giving away money
was a waste of time.</i>

1247
01:24:50.970 --> 01:24:56.932
<i>Under Jobs, Apple terminated
its philanthropic programs.</i>

1248
01:24:57.059 --> 01:25:00.177
<i>Jobs kept acting
as if Apple was a start-up,</i>

1249
01:25:00.354 --> 01:25:04.644
<i>but by 2010, it was one of the most
valuable companies in the world.</i>

1250
01:25:05.693 --> 01:25:09.232
<i>Among the rich and famous,
Jobs was a compelling character.</i>

1251
01:25:09.405 --> 01:25:14.742
<i>A counter-culture businessman.
But what were his values as a citizen?</i>

1252
01:25:14.869 --> 01:25:17.452
<i>Was he interested in power
to change the world</i>

1253
01:25:17.580 --> 01:25:20.823
<i>or the right to have power
without responsibility?</i>

1254
01:25:24.420 --> 01:25:27.128
<i>There was an experience
where actually a couple of people</i>

1255
01:25:27.256 --> 01:25:28.963
<i>were fighting over you,
were they not?</i>

1256
01:25:29.091 --> 01:25:32.004
- Oh, man. I went to Palm.
- Then a bunch of people went to Palm.

1257
01:25:32.136 --> 01:25:33.593
Yeah. Yeah.

1258
01:25:33.721 --> 01:25:36.384
- Was Steve pissed off?
- Oh, man. Yeah.

1259
01:25:36.515 --> 01:25:40.759
I gave my resignation.
It went up the chain, like you do.

1260
01:25:40.895 --> 01:25:45.265
And just sure as hell,
like 20 minutes later,

1261
01:25:45.441 --> 01:25:48.684
I get a call from Steve's admin,
"Steve wants to see you."

1262
01:25:49.904 --> 01:25:53.068
<i>He sits down. He just kind of sits
there, and he looks at me.</i>

1263
01:25:53.199 --> 01:25:56.613
And I start to kind of launch
into my little spiel that I had planned,

1264
01:25:56.744 --> 01:26:00.863
and he says, "You know
you fucked up Bluetooth, right?"

1265
01:26:00.998 --> 01:26:03.115
I just stopped. I'm like...

1266
01:26:03.334 --> 01:26:06.748
And then we go through
this half-hour mind fuck.

1267
01:26:06.879 --> 01:26:09.087
<i>It becomes very "Godfather" - esque.</i>

1268
01:26:09.215 --> 01:26:12.424
<i>You know, "You're part of my family,
and Apple's my family,</i>

1269
01:26:12.551 --> 01:26:14.258
<i>and you don't want to leave my family."</i>

1270
01:26:14.428 --> 01:26:18.342
And at the end, he says,
"If you choose to leave my family,

1271
01:26:18.516 --> 01:26:23.181
should you decide to take so much
as one member of my family away from me,

1272
01:26:23.312 --> 01:26:25.429
I will personally take you down."

1273
01:26:26.565 --> 01:26:28.602
<i>To keep his family together,</i>

1274
01:26:28.734 --> 01:26:33.570
<i>Jobs was willing to let Apple bend
or even break the law.</i>

1275
01:26:33.698 --> 01:26:36.862
<i>In 2011, a class-action lawsuit</i>

1276
01:26:36.992 --> 01:26:40.281
<i>filed by more than
64,000 Silicon Valley workers</i>

1277
01:26:40.454 --> 01:26:44.698
<i>revealed that Jobs, along with
the CEOs of Google, Intel and Adobe</i>

1278
01:26:44.834 --> 01:26:48.373
<i>had colluded not to recruit
each other's employees.</i>

1279
01:26:48.546 --> 01:26:51.664
If you're working there at Apple,
or wherever you're working,

1280
01:26:51.799 --> 01:26:54.758
you've got another company
that you might move to

1281
01:26:54.885 --> 01:26:57.798
and take your expertise with you
and earn more money at that company.

1282
01:26:57.930 --> 01:27:00.843
They won't accept your résumé.
They won't return your phone calls.

1283
01:27:00.975 --> 01:27:03.513
Right. Because they won't
let them poach each other.

1284
01:27:03.644 --> 01:27:06.057
- Correct.
- That's not a legitimate situation.

1285
01:27:06.188 --> 01:27:08.054
You know what some of
the strongest evidence was?

1286
01:27:08.190 --> 01:27:10.477
E-mails of the late, great Steve Jobs.

1287
01:27:10.609 --> 01:27:12.271
- Really?
- Tons of them, yes.

1288
01:27:17.742 --> 01:27:20.701
<i>Less than a month after
Google co-founder Sergey Brin</i>

1289
01:27:20.828 --> 01:27:22.865
<i>received this threat from Jobs,</i>

1290
01:27:22.997 --> 01:27:28.538
<i>Google circulated a "do not cold
call" list that included Apple.</i>

1291
01:27:28.669 --> 01:27:31.207
<i>Two years later, Google tried again,</i>

1292
01:27:31.338 --> 01:27:34.206
<i>and Jobs e-mailed Google CEO
Eric Schmidt</i>

1293
01:27:34.341 --> 01:27:36.754
<i>to remind him of their
gentlemen's agreement.</i>

1294
01:27:39.263 --> 01:27:41.596
<i>Schmidt placated Jobs by assuring him</i>

1295
01:27:41.724 --> 01:27:45.593
<i>that the culprit would be fired
within the hour.</i>

1296
01:27:45.728 --> 01:27:48.436
<i>When Jobs learned
that the woman had been canned,</i>

1297
01:27:48.689 --> 01:27:52.399
<i>he showed his pleasure
in two efficient keystrokes.</i>

1298
01:27:58.032 --> 01:28:01.275
Someone's going to come out
of the door. Do we want a shot of that?

1299
01:28:05.539 --> 01:28:08.031
Are you taking a picture
of the inside?

1300
01:28:08.167 --> 01:28:10.625
We're taking a picture
of the Apple logo on the door.

1301
01:28:10.753 --> 01:28:14.212
OK, we cannot have people
taking pictures.

1302
01:28:14.340 --> 01:28:16.081
<i>Tonight, a Wall Street scandal</i>

1303
01:28:16.217 --> 01:28:19.051
<i>has reached deep into
an iconic American company,</i>

1304
01:28:19.178 --> 01:28:20.714
<i>the Apple Corporation.</i>

1305
01:28:20.846 --> 01:28:23.304
It all centers on an alleged scheme

1306
01:28:23.432 --> 01:28:27.676
to under-report Apple's expenses
by $40 million,

1307
01:28:27.812 --> 01:28:29.553
including $20 million

1308
01:28:29.688 --> 01:28:33.227
that went straight to the company's
celebrity CEO Steve Jobs,

1309
01:28:33.400 --> 01:28:38.236
<i>in the form of what are known
as backdated options.</i>

1310
01:28:38.364 --> 01:28:42.904
I first met Steve Jobs shortly after
I became editor of <i>Fortune</i> magazine.

1311
01:28:43.953 --> 01:28:46.661
<i>And I said, "Listen, we'd love to have
a good relationship with Apple</i>

1312
01:28:46.789 --> 01:28:47.789
<i>and do stories about you."</i>

1313
01:28:47.915 --> 01:28:50.498
And he said, "Look,
this is how it's going to work."

1314
01:28:50.626 --> 01:28:52.788
"You know,
you want to do a story about us,

1315
01:28:52.920 --> 01:28:56.789
you call us up, propose it,
you know, we'll think about it."

1316
01:28:56.924 --> 01:28:59.792
"We'll basically
come up with the ideas with you,

1317
01:28:59.927 --> 01:29:02.169
or come up with the ideas,
we'll call you,

1318
01:29:02.304 --> 01:29:06.969
we'll figure out who the writer is going
to be on your staff to do the story."

1319
01:29:07.101 --> 01:29:11.220
And I said, "Well, you know, Steve,
that's not really how we do things."

1320
01:29:11.355 --> 01:29:13.438
And he goes, "That's how
you do things with Apple."

1321
01:29:14.441 --> 01:29:15.441
<i>So I say to myself,</i>

1322
01:29:15.526 --> 01:29:18.610
<i>"Why don't we do a story
about the stock options?"</i>

1323
01:29:18.737 --> 01:29:21.104
<i>"Because no one's
really figured it out."</i>

1324
01:29:21.240 --> 01:29:25.860
So, I decided to put one of our top
investigative reporters on the story,

1325
01:29:25.995 --> 01:29:26.995
Peter Elkind.

1326
01:29:27.121 --> 01:29:30.535
Steve Jobs had a very talented group
of key lieutenants around him.

1327
01:29:30.708 --> 01:29:34.998
<i>And he wanted give his people stock
option grants that were so big,</i>

1328
01:29:35.129 --> 01:29:37.337
<i>that they wouldn't even think about
going somewhere else</i>

1329
01:29:37.464 --> 01:29:39.376
<i>because the upside was so enormous.</i>

1330
01:29:39.508 --> 01:29:44.628
The key thing is if the stock goes up,

1331
01:29:44.763 --> 01:29:47.881
which we always hope it does,

1332
01:29:48.017 --> 01:29:53.308
then the golden handcuffs are
dramatically increased,

1333
01:29:53.439 --> 01:29:56.648
which is what I was hoping
would happen.

1334
01:29:57.526 --> 01:29:59.734
<i>To make those option deals
even sweeter,</i>

1335
01:29:59.862 --> 01:30:04.072
<i>companies would allow executives
to buy stock on dates in the past</i>

1336
01:30:04.199 --> 01:30:05.360
<i>when the price was low</i>

1337
01:30:05.492 --> 01:30:08.451
<i>so executives could make
millions in the blink of an eye.</i>

1338
01:30:08.579 --> 01:30:10.366
<i>This was called "backdating."</i>

1339
01:30:10.497 --> 01:30:12.329
<i>And it seemed like
the perfect solution,</i>

1340
01:30:12.541 --> 01:30:13.541
<i>except for one thing.</i>

1341
01:30:13.709 --> 01:30:16.952
<i>If not properly reported,
backdating is illegal.</i>

1342
01:30:17.087 --> 01:30:20.580
200 US public companies
are under investigation

1343
01:30:20.758 --> 01:30:24.923
over charges of backdated stock options
for their senior executives.

1344
01:30:26.305 --> 01:30:28.137
<i>Backdating was a dicey game,</i>

1345
01:30:28.265 --> 01:30:30.928
<i>and it landed several executives
in jail for fraud,</i>

1346
01:30:31.060 --> 01:30:34.553
<i>but it became a frequent practice
at Apple under Jobs.</i>

1347
01:30:34.730 --> 01:30:37.143
<i>Apple eventually conducted
an internal investigation</i>

1348
01:30:37.274 --> 01:30:38.560
<i>and found thousands of cases</i>

1349
01:30:38.734 --> 01:30:40.942
<i>where stock options had been
handled inappropriately.</i>

1350
01:30:41.070 --> 01:30:43.608
None of that had taken place
according to the company's own report

1351
01:30:43.864 --> 01:30:46.072
before Steve Jobs had returned there.

1352
01:30:46.200 --> 01:30:49.238
<i>A key advisor on many of
the troubled backdating schemes</i>

1353
01:30:49.370 --> 01:30:52.989
<i>was a powerful Silicon Valley attorney,
Larry Sonsini.</i>

1354
01:30:53.123 --> 01:30:55.206
<i>Larry Sonsini had kind of</i>

1355
01:30:55.334 --> 01:30:57.496
spread this dark art
in the world of Silicon Valley.

1356
01:30:57.628 --> 01:31:01.372
<i>He's the guy who whispered to the CEOs
of all the top tech companies,</i>

1357
01:31:01.548 --> 01:31:03.039
<i>"Here's how you can do this."</i>

1358
01:31:03.175 --> 01:31:05.918
<i>He was certainly very close
to Steve Jobs.</i>

1359
01:31:06.053 --> 01:31:09.546
<i>Sonsini had known Jobs
since Apple went public in 1980</i>

1360
01:31:09.723 --> 01:31:13.307
<i>and had been a board member at Pixar
when it created a vast program</i>

1361
01:31:13.435 --> 01:31:16.644
<i>of brazenly backdated options
for top executives.</i>

1362
01:31:16.814 --> 01:31:19.602
<i>Yet despite Jobs's long history
with backdating,</i>

1363
01:31:19.733 --> 01:31:23.101
<i>Apple's own investigation,
led by Al Gore,</i>

1364
01:31:23.237 --> 01:31:26.025
<i>absolved Jobs of any wrongdoing.</i>

1365
01:31:26.156 --> 01:31:28.864
<i>Their conclusion was that
Steve Jobs didn't appreciate</i>

1366
01:31:28.993 --> 01:31:31.531
<i>the accounting implications
of the issue.</i>

1367
01:31:31.662 --> 01:31:34.826
I know you're not an accountant,
but do you have an understanding

1368
01:31:34.999 --> 01:31:38.618
as to what generally accepted
accounting principles are?

1369
01:31:38.794 --> 01:31:40.456
Not really.

1370
01:31:40.587 --> 01:31:42.419
<i>Jobs's accounting naiveté</i>

1371
01:31:42.548 --> 01:31:45.416
<i>would be challenged by
his own CFO, Fred Anderson.</i>

1372
01:31:45.551 --> 01:31:48.009
<i>A hero during Apple's comeback,</i>

1373
01:31:48.137 --> 01:31:52.632
<i>Anderson was one exec who
took the fall for backdating.</i>

1374
01:31:52.808 --> 01:31:55.926
As Apple's chief financial officer,
Mr Anderson,

1375
01:31:56.061 --> 01:31:59.304
you had overall responsibility
to ensure that the company

1376
01:31:59.440 --> 01:32:03.104
complied with all financial
reporting requirements, true?

1377
01:32:03.235 --> 01:32:05.352
On advice of council,

1378
01:32:05.487 --> 01:32:09.572
I decline to answer based
on my Fifth Amendment rights.

1379
01:32:09.700 --> 01:32:11.282
<i>When the SEC investigated,</i>

1380
01:32:11.410 --> 01:32:14.619
<i>Apple effectively
threw Anderson under the bus.</i>

1381
01:32:14.788 --> 01:32:17.121
<i>He was forced to resign
from Apple's board</i>

1382
01:32:17.249 --> 01:32:20.413
<i>and to pay $3.6 million in penalties.</i>

1383
01:32:20.544 --> 01:32:24.288
<i>But in a very unusual statement,
Anderson's lawyer made it clear</i>

1384
01:32:24.423 --> 01:32:28.838
<i>that Fred had relied on statements
by Jobs that turned out to be false</i>

1385
01:32:28.969 --> 01:32:32.838
<i>and that Anderson had explained
the dangers of backdating to Jobs.</i>

1386
01:32:32.973 --> 01:32:37.559
Now, this contradicted exactly what
Jobs and the company had maintained

1387
01:32:37.686 --> 01:32:40.224
which was that Steve Jobs didn't
appreciate why this was a problem.

1388
01:32:40.355 --> 01:32:42.938
<i>I think the notion
that Steve Jobs knew nothing</i>

1389
01:32:43.067 --> 01:32:45.605
<i>and Fred Anderson and Nancy Heinen
were entirely responsible</i>

1390
01:32:45.861 --> 01:32:46.861
<i>is ridiculous.</i>

1391
01:32:47.988 --> 01:32:49.354
<i>The company has confirmed</i>

1392
01:32:49.490 --> 01:32:52.483
<i>that Jobs himself
was awarded backdated options</i>

1393
01:32:52.618 --> 01:32:55.611
<i>that carried a false date
in October 2001.</i>

1394
01:32:55.746 --> 01:32:58.580
<i>According to Apple's own report
to the SEC,</i>

1395
01:32:58.707 --> 01:33:03.168
<i>The award was "improperly recorded
as occurring at a special board meeting</i>

1396
01:33:03.295 --> 01:33:06.879
<i>when in fact such a board meeting
did not occur."</i>

1397
01:33:07.007 --> 01:33:12.969
<i>A fictitious board meeting
that awarded Jobs 7.5 million options.</i>

1398
01:33:13.097 --> 01:33:15.054
<i>Just what was going on?</i>

1399
01:33:15.182 --> 01:33:17.174
<i>At first, all fingers pointed</i>

1400
01:33:17.309 --> 01:33:19.972
<i>to another member
of the Apple comeback team,</i>

1401
01:33:20.104 --> 01:33:21.766
<i>General Counsel Nancy Heinen,</i>

1402
01:33:21.939 --> 01:33:25.523
<i>who had certified the minutes
of the phony board meeting.</i>

1403
01:33:25.651 --> 01:33:28.894
<i>Like Anderson,
Heinen would settle with the SEC</i>

1404
01:33:29.029 --> 01:33:31.692
<i>without admitting or denying guilt.</i>

1405
01:33:31.865 --> 01:33:37.156
<i>But her silence raised questions.
Would she have just decided on her own</i>

1406
01:33:37.287 --> 01:33:41.907
<i>to fake a board meeting
in order to enrich Steve Jobs?</i>

1407
01:33:42.042 --> 01:33:44.079
<i>Who wanted this done?</i>

1408
01:33:45.587 --> 01:33:51.083
As we've seen in the discussions
of the past hour,

1409
01:33:51.218 --> 01:33:55.963
I spent a lot of time trying
to take care of people at Apple

1410
01:33:56.098 --> 01:34:02.971
and to, you know,
surprise and delight them

1411
01:34:03.105 --> 01:34:08.351
with what a career at Apple
could mean to them and their families.

1412
01:34:09.319 --> 01:34:13.359
And I felt that the board wasn't
really doing the same with me.

1413
01:34:13.490 --> 01:34:17.325
- Right.
- So I was...

1414
01:34:20.706 --> 01:34:24.575
...hurt, I suppose would be
the most accurate word.

1415
01:34:24.710 --> 01:34:27.999
I'd been working, you know,
I don't know,

1416
01:34:28.130 --> 01:34:30.622
four years, five years of my life,

1417
01:34:30.757 --> 01:34:34.592
and not seeing my family
very much and stuff.

1418
01:34:34.720 --> 01:34:37.178
And I just felt like

1419
01:34:37.306 --> 01:34:40.140
there's nobody looking out
for me here, you know?

1420
01:34:40.267 --> 01:34:41.428
Right. OK.

1421
01:34:41.560 --> 01:34:47.852
So, I wanted them to do something,
and so we talked about it.

1422
01:34:48.025 --> 01:34:51.143
<i>There's no question that
the directives came from him.</i>

1423
01:34:51.278 --> 01:34:53.611
<i>Yet, when the SEC investigated,</i>

1424
01:34:53.739 --> 01:34:56.982
it was as if he was immune.

1425
01:34:58.035 --> 01:35:01.949
I'd wished they'd have come to me
and said,

1426
01:35:02.080 --> 01:35:05.699
"Steve, we've got this new grant
for you,"

1427
01:35:05.834 --> 01:35:08.702
without me having to suggest anything

1428
01:35:08.837 --> 01:35:12.956
or be involved in anything
or negotiate anything.

1429
01:35:13.091 --> 01:35:16.129
That would have been much better
from the company's point of view

1430
01:35:16.261 --> 01:35:23.054
because it would have made me
feel better at that time.

1431
01:35:24.895 --> 01:35:26.682
<i>According to one analyst,</i>

1432
01:35:26.813 --> 01:35:28.770
<i>if Jobs had gone to jail
for backdating,</i>

1433
01:35:28.899 --> 01:35:32.859
<i>the company's value would have
dropped by $22 billion.</i>

1434
01:35:35.530 --> 01:35:37.988
<i>But Jobs was Apple's indispensable man,</i>

1435
01:35:38.116 --> 01:35:43.362
<i>and Apple, Silicon Valley's
indispensable company.</i>

1436
01:35:43.497 --> 01:35:46.990
<i>A cornerstone
of the entire American economy.</i>

1437
01:35:47.125 --> 01:35:49.868
<i>But just how American was Apple?</i>

1438
01:35:51.505 --> 01:35:54.248
Do you swear that the testimony
you're about to give will be...

1439
01:35:54.383 --> 01:35:56.841
<i>When the US Senate
questioned Jobs's successor,</i>

1440
01:35:57.010 --> 01:35:59.502
<i>Tim Cook,
about the company's tax practices,</i>

1441
01:35:59.638 --> 01:36:03.097
<i>many of the subcommittee members
took a moment to state,</i>

1442
01:36:03.225 --> 01:36:08.095
<i>for the record, just how tough
they were willing to be.</i>

1443
01:36:08.230 --> 01:36:13.771
I love Apple, and I'm very proud
of Apple as an American company.

1444
01:36:13.902 --> 01:36:17.316
Apple is an American success story.

1445
01:36:17.531 --> 01:36:22.401
Its products are justifiably well-known
and used throughout the world.

1446
01:36:22.536 --> 01:36:25.870
Just like millions around the world,
I carry an iPhone in my pocket.

1447
01:36:26.039 --> 01:36:27.871
What may not be so well-known

1448
01:36:28.041 --> 01:36:32.411
is that Apple also has a highly
developed tax-avoidance system,

1449
01:36:32.546 --> 01:36:36.586
a system through which it has
amassed more than $100 billion

1450
01:36:36.717 --> 01:36:39.630
in off-shore cash in a tax haven.

1451
01:36:41.805 --> 01:36:45.173
<i>Apple found its tax haven
in the green fields of Ireland</i>

1452
01:36:45.309 --> 01:36:49.303
<i>where Jobs and his team set up
holding companies in the early 1980s.</i>

1453
01:36:49.438 --> 01:36:52.476
<i>The scheme is known as
a "Double Irish".</i>

1454
01:36:53.692 --> 01:36:55.399
<i>Holly Hill industrial estate</i>

1455
01:36:55.527 --> 01:36:58.235
<i>can scarcely be compared
to California's Silicon Valley.</i>

1456
01:36:58.363 --> 01:37:02.573
<i>There is the feeling that Apple of Cork
may become something of a Silicon Hill.</i>

1457
01:37:07.414 --> 01:37:12.955
<i>Today, Apple holds more than
$137 billion of its profits overseas.</i>

1458
01:37:13.128 --> 01:37:18.089
<i>Much of it in two small Irish
companies, one with no employees.</i>

1459
01:37:18.216 --> 01:37:22.381
<i>While the actual cash is held
and invested by New York banks,</i>

1460
01:37:22.512 --> 01:37:26.347
<i>the paper profits are steered
through an office park in Reno, Nevada,</i>

1461
01:37:26.475 --> 01:37:31.266
<i>and then back to the Emerald Isle
where it's taxed at rates less than 1%.</i>

1462
01:37:32.397 --> 01:37:34.605
Senator, we're proud
that all of our R&D,

1463
01:37:34.733 --> 01:37:36.816
or the vast majority of it,
is in the United States.

1464
01:37:36.943 --> 01:37:40.027
I know, but the profits that
result from it are sitting in Ireland

1465
01:37:40.197 --> 01:37:43.861
in corporations that you control
that don't pay taxes.

1466
01:37:44.034 --> 01:37:48.495
Part of the mythology
of these new companies

1467
01:37:48.622 --> 01:37:53.708
<i>was that they in some way reflected
something about America.</i>

1468
01:37:55.128 --> 01:37:58.838
<i>Both Google and Apple
have played on the idea</i>

1469
01:37:58.965 --> 01:38:02.254
<i>of political virtue as part
of selling their company.</i>

1470
01:38:02.386 --> 01:38:09.179
So then to find out they weren't about
political virtue is distressing.

1471
01:38:14.064 --> 01:38:19.025
<i>Steve Jobs said he wanted
to change the world, but into what?</i>

1472
01:38:20.153 --> 01:38:23.772
<i>Companies all over the world
make choices on how to treat workers,</i>

1473
01:38:23.907 --> 01:38:26.900
<i>what to give back
and where to put their money.</i>

1474
01:38:27.035 --> 01:38:29.197
<i>What were Steve's choices?</i>

1475
01:38:29.329 --> 01:38:32.447
<i>In meditation, he found simplicity.</i>

1476
01:38:32.582 --> 01:38:38.203
<i>He loved the idea of "Be Here Now."
But where was "here?"</i>

1477
01:38:39.756 --> 01:38:42.874
<i>Ladies and gentlemen,
we have just landed</i>

1478
01:38:43.009 --> 01:38:46.093
<i>at Beijing International Airport.</i>

1479
01:38:53.478 --> 01:38:56.687
<i>I thought it was important
to cover China</i>

1480
01:38:56.815 --> 01:39:01.276
because Apple has
all of its products made there.

1481
01:39:01.403 --> 01:39:04.316
<i>I mean,
they design their own products,</i>

1482
01:39:04.448 --> 01:39:06.781
<i>but the manufacturing is done in China,</i>

1483
01:39:06.908 --> 01:39:10.777
<i>and so it's caught up in another
country's industrial revolution.</i>

1484
01:39:10.912 --> 01:39:13.655
And a lot of that is
out of Apple's control.

1485
01:39:19.671 --> 01:39:22.709
<i>Four workers died
and 77 were wounded</i>

1486
01:39:22.841 --> 01:39:25.925
<i>in explosions
at two Apple supplier factories</i>

1487
01:39:26.052 --> 01:39:28.135
<i>caused by careless safety procedures.</i>

1488
01:39:29.514 --> 01:39:31.756
<i>The solvents used to sparkle
Apple's touch screens</i>

1489
01:39:31.892 --> 01:39:35.385
<i>were powerful but dangerous,
causing nerve damage</i>

1490
01:39:35.520 --> 01:39:39.434
<i>that led to weakness
and loss of touch in workers.</i>

1491
01:39:39.566 --> 01:39:44.311
<i>They complained about low wages
and pressure to meet Apple's deadlines.</i>

1492
01:39:45.363 --> 01:39:48.151
<i>In the Chinese factories
of many tech companies,</i>

1493
01:39:48.325 --> 01:39:50.282
<i>copper, chromium
and other heavy metals</i>

1494
01:39:50.410 --> 01:39:54.370
<i>saturate the run-off that flows
into local waterways.</i>

1495
01:39:54.498 --> 01:39:56.660
<i>Sometimes chemical levels
are so high</i>

1496
01:39:56.791 --> 01:39:59.625
<i>that sewage treatment plants
can't adequately clean the water</i>

1497
01:39:59.753 --> 01:40:01.836
<i>for it ever to be used again.</i>

1498
01:40:03.924 --> 01:40:09.420
<i>In 2010, Chinese activist Ma Jun
contacted all the tech manufacturers</i>

1499
01:40:09.554 --> 01:40:13.514
<i>to discuss the issue
and even wrote to Jobs personally.</i>

1500
01:40:13.642 --> 01:40:17.932
<i>All the companies ultimately responded
except one. Apple.</i>

1501
01:40:18.063 --> 01:40:20.726
<i>It wasn't until Jobs left the company</i>

1502
01:40:20.857 --> 01:40:24.066
<i>that Apple even agreed
to speak to Ma Jun.</i>

1503
01:40:29.991 --> 01:40:32.278
<i>Foxconn is Apple's top supplier,</i>

1504
01:40:32.410 --> 01:40:36.780
so it all goes downhill from there
in terms of the standards,

1505
01:40:36.915 --> 01:40:38.497
in terms of everything.

1506
01:40:40.544 --> 01:40:43.662
<i>These young people
come from the countryside</i>

1507
01:40:43.797 --> 01:40:49.293
<i>under unimaginably poor conditions,
looking for a better life.</i>

1508
01:40:54.182 --> 01:40:56.925
<i>Sun Danyong, nicknamed Yong,</i>

1509
01:40:57.060 --> 01:40:59.848
<i>came to Foxconn
from a small mountain village.</i>

1510
01:41:02.482 --> 01:41:04.690
<i>Placed in the product
communications department,</i>

1511
01:41:04.818 --> 01:41:08.186
<i>he was responsible for the security
of iPhone prototypes</i>

1512
01:41:08.446 --> 01:41:10.938
<i>bound for Apple's headquarters
in Silicon Valley.</i>

1513
01:41:11.074 --> 01:41:17.366
<i>In 2009, an iPhone 4 prototype
went missing on Yong's watch.</i>

1514
01:41:24.754 --> 01:41:28.498
<i>Sun Danyong was
the first factory worker</i>

1515
01:41:28.633 --> 01:41:31.046
<i>who came under the spotlight.</i>

1516
01:41:31.177 --> 01:41:37.390
What makes his story incredibly vivid
is that in today's day and age,

1517
01:41:37.517 --> 01:41:41.056
there's CCTV cameras recording
where his movements were.

1518
01:41:41.187 --> 01:41:45.807
<i>He had a conversation with his friends
on the Chinese Twitter site,</i>

1519
01:41:45.942 --> 01:41:49.856
and you could really track
what had happened.

1520
01:41:50.864 --> 01:41:53.026
<i>Yong searched
high and low for the prototype</i>

1521
01:41:53.241 --> 01:41:56.405
<i>before reporting it missing
to Foxconn security.</i>

1522
01:41:57.120 --> 01:42:00.284
<i>That evening, officials took him
into an interrogation room</i>

1523
01:42:00.457 --> 01:42:01.914
<i>where he was assaulted</i>

1524
01:42:02.042 --> 01:42:05.626
<i>and was told police would arrive
the next morning to question him.</i>

1525
01:42:06.671 --> 01:42:10.961
<i>Yong was finally permitted
to leave the factory at 10:41pm.</i>

1526
01:42:11.092 --> 01:42:13.459
<i>He wandered to an internet cafe</i>

1527
01:42:13.595 --> 01:42:16.804
<i>where he chatted online
with his friends.</i>

1528
01:42:32.781 --> 01:42:35.649
<i>He logged off
around 1:30 in the morning.</i>

1529
01:42:37.035 --> 01:42:39.903
<i>Soon after, security cameras
spotted him in an elevator</i>

1530
01:42:40.038 --> 01:42:42.826
<i>in his apartment complex.</i>

1531
01:42:42.957 --> 01:42:46.200
<i>He got out on the 12th floor
and texted his girlfriend.</i>

1532
01:43:02.936 --> 01:43:09.729
<i>At 3:33am, security cameras recorded
Sun Danyong jumping to his death.</i>

1533
01:43:15.657 --> 01:43:19.526
<i>Over a period of two years or so,
there were 18 suicides.</i>

1534
01:43:19.661 --> 01:43:24.998
Foxconn set up these nets
to catch people who fall off.

1535
01:43:25.125 --> 01:43:27.412
<i>People were jumping
off of the buildings,</i>

1536
01:43:27.544 --> 01:43:29.831
<i>so they were going to
prevent people from dying</i>

1537
01:43:29.963 --> 01:43:33.001
<i>by having safety nets to catch them.</i>

1538
01:43:38.972 --> 01:43:41.806
You know, they've had,
if you count the attempted suicides,

1539
01:43:41.933 --> 01:43:43.890
13 so far this year.

1540
01:43:44.018 --> 01:43:49.730
And while that is still...
They have 400,000 people at this place.

1541
01:43:49.858 --> 01:43:53.852
So, 13 out of 400,000
is 26 per year so far.

1542
01:43:53.987 --> 01:44:00.109
For 400,000 people or, you know,
let's say seven per 100,000 people,

1543
01:44:00.243 --> 01:44:04.328
that's still under the US suicide rate
of 11 per 100,000 people,

1544
01:44:04.497 --> 01:44:06.113
but it's really troubling.

1545
01:44:06.249 --> 01:44:08.161
Right. It's in one place, too.

1546
01:44:08.293 --> 01:44:10.706
Well, you measure it
by number of people.

1547
01:44:10.920 --> 01:44:14.834
You measure it by numbers of people.
So, we're all over this.

1548
01:44:14.966 --> 01:44:18.710
And it's... It's very troubling.

1549
01:44:18.845 --> 01:44:22.338
So, we're over there trying
to understand what's happening,

1550
01:44:22.515 --> 01:44:25.007
and more importantly,
trying to understand how we can help.

1551
01:44:31.024 --> 01:44:34.734
<i>Apple isn't the only company
to manufacture in China,</i>

1552
01:44:34.903 --> 01:44:38.817
<i>but it is different in one way.
Its enormous profit margin.</i>

1553
01:44:38.948 --> 01:44:43.033
<i>The profit on every iPhone 4
was over $300.</i>

1554
01:44:43.203 --> 01:44:48.119
<i>Yet Apple paid its Chinese workforce
less than $12 per phone.</i>

1555
01:44:48.249 --> 01:44:51.959
<i>If Jobs had really "thought different,"
shouldn't he have cared more</i>

1556
01:44:52.086 --> 01:44:53.918
<i>about the people
who touched the iPhones</i>

1557
01:44:54.047 --> 01:44:58.041
<i>before they appeared in
the hands of Apple's customers?</i>

1558
01:45:06.142 --> 01:45:09.977
<i>When I was writing
critical stories about Apple,</i>

1559
01:45:10.104 --> 01:45:13.268
the mail would be 80% hate mail.

1560
01:45:15.568 --> 01:45:21.155
<i>Even the most reasoned,
judicious criticism</i>

1561
01:45:21.282 --> 01:45:27.404
<i>about labor practices in China,
for crying out loud, it didn't matter.</i>

1562
01:45:27.580 --> 01:45:30.072
<i>People didn't want to hear it.</i>

1563
01:45:30.208 --> 01:45:33.952
<i>They loved this company.
They loved its products.</i>

1564
01:45:34.087 --> 01:45:37.706
<i>They loved the status symbol
of having these things in their hand</i>

1565
01:45:37.841 --> 01:45:41.255
<i>and looking at it all the time,
and it just felt cool,</i>

1566
01:45:41.386 --> 01:45:46.131
<i>and they'd stood in line for two days
to buy one,</i>

1567
01:45:46.266 --> 01:45:48.679
<i>and they didn't want to hear it.</i>

1568
01:45:54.440 --> 01:45:57.774
<i>I was one of those people
who had to have an iPhone.</i>

1569
01:45:57.902 --> 01:46:00.610
<i>I didn't want
to hear about other products,</i>

1570
01:46:00.738 --> 01:46:02.479
<i>and I believed against all reason</i>

1571
01:46:02.657 --> 01:46:06.025
<i>that owning an iPhone made me
part of something better.</i>

1572
01:46:06.160 --> 01:46:09.995
<i>And when it was in my pocket,
for every idle moment,</i>

1573
01:46:10.123 --> 01:46:14.538
<i>my hand was drawn to it,
like Frodo's hand to the ring.</i>

1574
01:46:15.420 --> 01:46:18.413
<i>The real magic of it
is that these myths</i>

1575
01:46:18.548 --> 01:46:24.010
<i>are surrounding a company
that makes phones.</i>

1576
01:46:25.513 --> 01:46:27.926
A phone is not a mythical device.

1577
01:46:29.100 --> 01:46:31.092
Um...

1578
01:46:31.227 --> 01:46:38.851
And it sort of makes you wonder
less about Apple than about us.

1579
01:46:40.570 --> 01:46:43.859
<i>The myth-making
around technology in general</i>

1580
01:46:43.990 --> 01:46:50.863
<i>allows the technologist to do things
that would be viewed as heinous</i>

1581
01:46:50.997 --> 01:46:54.161
<i>if they were done
by other kinds of companies.</i>

1582
01:46:56.169 --> 01:46:58.411
<i>This is a story that's amazing.</i>

1583
01:46:58.546 --> 01:47:03.041
<i>It's got theft, it's got buying
stolen property, it's got extortion.</i>

1584
01:47:03.176 --> 01:47:05.839
I'm sure there's sex
in there somewhere, you know?

1585
01:47:05.970 --> 01:47:07.336
Really?

1586
01:47:07.472 --> 01:47:10.465
So somebody should make
a movie out of this.

1587
01:47:10.600 --> 01:47:13.058
<i>If you were to make a movie
about it,</i>

1588
01:47:13.186 --> 01:47:16.679
<i>the first scene would be set in
a beer garden in Silicon Valley.</i>

1589
01:47:16.814 --> 01:47:19.602
<i>As he swilled a few steins of Pilsner,</i>

1590
01:47:19.776 --> 01:47:23.235
<i>Apple's Gray Powell was testing out
a new iPhone prototype.</i>

1591
01:47:25.114 --> 01:47:28.607
<i>But when Powell staggered
out of the bar, he forgot one thing.</i>

1592
01:47:28.785 --> 01:47:30.526
<i>The iPhone.</i>

1593
01:47:30.703 --> 01:47:35.823
<i>It was found on a bar stool
by a college student named Brian Hogan.</i>

1594
01:47:37.168 --> 01:47:39.080
<i>And this e-mail
comes into our tip box,</i>

1595
01:47:39.212 --> 01:47:43.547
and there was this guy claiming
that he had this new iPhone prototype.

1596
01:47:43.716 --> 01:47:47.881
<i>Getting inside Apple's security fort,</i>

1597
01:47:48.012 --> 01:47:50.470
<i>and look at something
that is under wrap.</i>

1598
01:47:50.598 --> 01:47:54.433
But back then, when Steve Jobs was
at the helm and in his full power,

1599
01:47:54.560 --> 01:47:56.552
it was impossible
to get anything from them.

1600
01:47:56.729 --> 01:47:58.641
It was incredibly exciting.

1601
01:47:58.815 --> 01:48:01.728
At that point, Apple didn't have
a whole lot of leaks.

1602
01:48:01.859 --> 01:48:04.852
And then I go to Nick and I say,

1603
01:48:04.988 --> 01:48:08.072
"We think it's the real thing,
and they want their money."

1604
01:48:08.199 --> 01:48:10.191
And Nick said, "Anything you want."

1605
01:48:10.326 --> 01:48:14.115
For us, there's no question
as to whether we write the story or not.

1606
01:48:14.247 --> 01:48:17.411
That's what was so disturbing,
I think, to Steve Jobs,

1607
01:48:17.542 --> 01:48:19.784
was that he'd been used to having

1608
01:48:19.919 --> 01:48:22.332
a much more controlled
relationship with the press.

1609
01:48:22.463 --> 01:48:25.171
Our plan was to take pictures of it,

1610
01:48:25.299 --> 01:48:28.508
write about it,
and then return it back to Apple.

1611
01:48:28.636 --> 01:48:31.174
Hey, I'm Jason Chen.
This is the new iPhone.

1612
01:48:31.305 --> 01:48:33.342
Here are some of the new features.

1613
01:48:33.474 --> 01:48:36.888
<i>In the beginning, it was OK.
Only nerds looking at it, I guess.</i>

1614
01:48:37.020 --> 01:48:38.431
And then the story
started to pick up.

1615
01:48:40.523 --> 01:48:43.857
You know, it was the biggest
scoop in tech in history.

1616
01:48:46.237 --> 01:48:49.321
"Hi, this is Steve Jobs.
I want my phone back."

1617
01:48:49.449 --> 01:48:51.566
And it was
in a really charming voice.

1618
01:48:51.701 --> 01:48:55.945
And it's same way you'd ask for,
you know, a hat you'd lent a friend.

1619
01:48:56.080 --> 01:49:00.791
<i>I'd met him a couple of times before.
It was his voice, unmistakably.</i>

1620
01:49:00.918 --> 01:49:02.409
He said, "You know, I'm not mad at you."

1621
01:49:02.545 --> 01:49:04.582
"It's someone we worked with
who lost it."

1622
01:49:04.714 --> 01:49:06.706
"But you've had your fun,
and we need this phone back

1623
01:49:06.883 --> 01:49:08.499
before it gets into the wrong hands."

1624
01:49:08.634 --> 01:49:12.127
And at that point, I was thinking,
"Isn't it already in the wrong hands?"

1625
01:49:17.560 --> 01:49:21.895
Nick at that moment said,
"Ask for a letter."

1626
01:49:22.023 --> 01:49:24.015
"Ask for an official letter
asking for it."

1627
01:49:24.150 --> 01:49:26.688
"We need the actual confirmation
that this is the real thing."

1628
01:49:26.903 --> 01:49:29.941
<i>The next call,
he said he didn't want to claim it.</i>

1629
01:49:30.073 --> 01:49:32.235
<i>He really changed his tone
at that point,</i>

1630
01:49:32.366 --> 01:49:34.983
<i>because it would affect
the sales of the current model,</i>

1631
01:49:35.203 --> 01:49:36.910
which is kind of disappointing,
you know?

1632
01:49:37.038 --> 01:49:39.530
You hear all these stories about
this guy not caring about money.

1633
01:49:39.665 --> 01:49:42.078
<i>And he goes,
"This is some serious shit."</i>

1634
01:49:42.210 --> 01:49:45.123
<i>"If I have to serve you papers,
I'm coming for something,</i>

1635
01:49:45.254 --> 01:49:48.088
<i>and it's going to mean someone in your
organization is going to go to jail."</i>

1636
01:49:48.216 --> 01:49:51.300
For a reporter who's got a chip
on his shoulder against corporations,

1637
01:49:51.427 --> 01:49:55.046
that's like,
"Martyr me. Please, martyr me."

1638
01:49:55.181 --> 01:49:59.266
"I'll go to jail for an iPhone.
Like, really."

1639
01:49:59.393 --> 01:50:03.228
<i>He called back later and he said,
"OK, we'll get you the letter,"</i>

1640
01:50:03.356 --> 01:50:05.564
<i>and he was just resigned and cold.</i>

1641
01:50:05.691 --> 01:50:08.729
<i>So, they sent us the letter,
and they sent a lawyer from Apple</i>

1642
01:50:08.903 --> 01:50:11.236
to Jason's house to pick up the phone.

1643
01:50:11.364 --> 01:50:13.572
It was a very cold exchange.

1644
01:50:13.741 --> 01:50:16.279
He said, "I believe you have
something of mine," or something.

1645
01:50:16.410 --> 01:50:19.494
And I handed it to him, and he said,
"Thank you very much," and he left.

1646
01:50:19.622 --> 01:50:22.490
Was that the end of it,
as far as Apple was concerned?

1647
01:50:22.625 --> 01:50:23.957
No, of course not.

1648
01:50:24.085 --> 01:50:27.704
Then all the nightmares started.

1649
01:50:54.782 --> 01:50:58.526
The cops had to bash in the guy's door?

1650
01:50:58.661 --> 01:51:01.950
Don't they know there's an app for that?

1651
01:51:02.081 --> 01:51:04.448
<i>Anyone who'd worked
with Jobs before</i>

1652
01:51:04.584 --> 01:51:07.702
would know of other instances
where he'd been a bully.

1653
01:51:07.837 --> 01:51:10.625
But this was probably
the most public evidence of bullying.

1654
01:51:11.841 --> 01:51:15.460
<i>My wife and I went out for dinner.
We came back home,</i>

1655
01:51:15.595 --> 01:51:18.133
<i>and we noticed the garage door
was slightly opened.</i>

1656
01:51:18.264 --> 01:51:21.632
You know, and I was wondering,
"What's going on?"

1657
01:51:21.767 --> 01:51:24.510
<i>And I opened it all the way,
and I noticed there were people inside.</i>

1658
01:51:24.687 --> 01:51:28.180
<i>And I thought, you know,
"Holy crap, I'm being robbed."</i>

1659
01:51:28.316 --> 01:51:30.774
And then I looked closer
and realized it was cops.

1660
01:51:33.070 --> 01:51:35.778
<i>The cops seized boxes
of Chen's personal property</i>

1661
01:51:35.948 --> 01:51:38.691
<i>including four computers,
two cellphones</i>

1662
01:51:38.826 --> 01:51:41.409
<i>and a box of his business cards.</i>

1663
01:51:43.164 --> 01:51:48.205
<i>For the Gizmodo movie, this raised
questions of plot and motivation.</i>

1664
01:51:48.336 --> 01:51:53.252
<i>Why break down Chen's door
after he returned the iPhone?</i>

1665
01:51:53.382 --> 01:51:57.422
<i>They showed me the warrant
to search the premises and said,</i>

1666
01:51:57.553 --> 01:51:59.340
"We're part of the REACT team."

1667
01:51:59.472 --> 01:52:02.590
<i>After they searched the house,
obviously I went and Googled it.</i>

1668
01:52:02.725 --> 01:52:04.762
<i>The officers
who raided Chen's apartment</i>

1669
01:52:04.894 --> 01:52:08.433
<i>were part of a little-known criminal
task force called REACT,</i>

1670
01:52:08.564 --> 01:52:11.773
<i>composed of local, state
and federal officials</i>

1671
01:52:11.901 --> 01:52:15.394
<i>on the lookout for corporate espionage
in Silicon Valley.</i>

1672
01:52:17.406 --> 01:52:20.319
My initial response was,
"This is cool. It's Apple."

1673
01:52:21.410 --> 01:52:23.197
<i>Chris Feasel
was the deputy DA</i>

1674
01:52:23.329 --> 01:52:25.867
<i>advising REACT on the case.</i>

1675
01:52:26.040 --> 01:52:28.032
<i>After the raid on Chen's apartment,</i>

1676
01:52:28.167 --> 01:52:31.501
<i>Feasel was received at Apple
by Jobs himself.</i>

1677
01:52:31.629 --> 01:52:35.669
He was very, very nice,
very high-energy.

1678
01:52:36.634 --> 01:52:39.718
<i>We had a back-and-forth about
what he wanted to see happen</i>

1679
01:52:39.845 --> 01:52:42.337
<i>versus what some of the realities were
about doing a prosecution.</i>

1680
01:52:42.473 --> 01:52:46.592
He was very supportive about whatever
choices that we made on the case.

1681
01:52:46.727 --> 01:52:48.764
Where do people
come down on this?

1682
01:52:48.896 --> 01:52:51.809
- Where do you come down on it?
- Well, I can just tell you what...

1683
01:52:51.941 --> 01:52:58.279
There is an ongoing investigation
by the DA, and I'm not current on it.

1684
01:52:58.406 --> 01:53:01.945
He was very involved in it
and very interested in it

1685
01:53:02.076 --> 01:53:03.658
and wanted to be kept abreast

1686
01:53:03.786 --> 01:53:06.028
about what was going on
in the investigation.

1687
01:53:06.956 --> 01:53:10.449
<i>Jobs had every reason to
expect that he would be kept informed</i>

1688
01:53:10.584 --> 01:53:13.622
<i>because REACT wasn't
a purely government agency.</i>

1689
01:53:13.754 --> 01:53:15.086
<i>It had a steering committee</i>

1690
01:53:15.256 --> 01:53:19.216
<i>composed of many of the major
companies in the Valley.</i>

1691
01:53:19.385 --> 01:53:22.799
<i>In a town so completely dominated
by the tech industry,</i>

1692
01:53:22.930 --> 01:53:25.297
<i>had law enforcement become the muscle</i>

1693
01:53:25.433 --> 01:53:27.846
<i>for the largest corporations
in the world?</i>

1694
01:53:31.272 --> 01:53:38.361
<i>He was very, very adamant
and very passionate about his creation.</i>

1695
01:53:38.487 --> 01:53:42.151
And the only analogy I can think of is
if somebody stole your baby,

1696
01:53:42.325 --> 01:53:44.191
you would be very upset about it.

1697
01:53:44.410 --> 01:53:48.871
<i>That's how Mr Jobs felt.
Somebody had taken his baby.</i>

1698
01:53:51.375 --> 01:53:53.332
<i>In spite of pressure
from Apple,</i>

1699
01:53:53.461 --> 01:53:56.454
<i>the DA decided
not to pursue the charges against Chen</i>

1700
01:53:56.589 --> 01:53:59.252
<i>because he hadn't received
stolen property.</i>

1701
01:53:59.383 --> 01:54:01.591
<i>He was a journalist doing a story.</i>

1702
01:54:02.970 --> 01:54:06.805
When this whole thing
with Gizmodo happened,

1703
01:54:06.932 --> 01:54:11.723
I got a lot of advice
from people that said,

1704
01:54:11.854 --> 01:54:18.146
"You shouldn't go after a journalist
because they bought stolen property,

1705
01:54:18.277 --> 01:54:22.567
and they tried to extort you.
You should let it slide."

1706
01:54:22.698 --> 01:54:27.113
"Apple's a big company now.
You don't want the PR."

1707
01:54:27.244 --> 01:54:29.201
"You should let it slide."

1708
01:54:29.330 --> 01:54:34.496
And I thought deeply about this,
and I ended up concluding

1709
01:54:34.627 --> 01:54:37.711
that the worst thing
that could possibly happen

1710
01:54:37.838 --> 01:54:42.333
as we get big and we get
a little more influence in the world,

1711
01:54:42.468 --> 01:54:48.886
is if we change our core values
and start letting it slide.

1712
01:54:49.058 --> 01:54:53.223
I can't do that. I'd rather quit.

1713
01:54:54.313 --> 01:54:57.351
<i>What values
was Jobs talking about?</i>

1714
01:54:57.483 --> 01:55:02.478
<i>When Apple was taking on IBM,
it was David versus Goliath.</i>

1715
01:55:02.655 --> 01:55:08.026
<i>But when Apple became Goliath,
to whom was Jobs giving the finger?</i>

1716
01:55:12.665 --> 01:55:17.501
<i>The sad thing is that how many
months did he have left after that?</i>

1717
01:55:17.628 --> 01:55:22.999
This was a guy who knew,
who knew at the time,

1718
01:55:23.175 --> 01:55:30.173
he was dying, and he dedicated,
what, ten minutes of his life

1719
01:55:30.307 --> 01:55:33.345
to talk about these guys
who found a phone in a bar

1720
01:55:33.477 --> 01:55:38.268
and then published a story about it?
Isn't that a little bit strange?

1721
01:55:41.444 --> 01:55:45.028
My third story is about death.

1722
01:55:46.073 --> 01:55:50.067
About a year ago,
I was diagnosed with cancer.

1723
01:55:50.244 --> 01:55:52.236
I had a scan at 7:30 in the morning,

1724
01:55:52.371 --> 01:55:55.455
and it clearly showed
a tumor on my pancreas.

1725
01:55:55.583 --> 01:55:57.575
I didn't even know
what a pancreas was.

1726
01:55:57.710 --> 01:55:59.292
The doctors told me

1727
01:55:59.420 --> 01:56:03.414
this was almost certainly
a type of cancer that is incurable,

1728
01:56:03.549 --> 01:56:07.634
and that I should expect to live
no longer than three to six months.

1729
01:56:07.761 --> 01:56:12.347
My doctor advised me to go home
and get my affairs in order,

1730
01:56:12.516 --> 01:56:15.850
which is doctor's code
for "prepare to die."

1731
01:56:15.978 --> 01:56:17.765
It means to try and tell your kids

1732
01:56:17.897 --> 01:56:21.732
everything you thought you'd have
the next ten years to tell them

1733
01:56:21.859 --> 01:56:23.316
in just a few months.

1734
01:56:23.444 --> 01:56:28.610
I lived with that diagnosis all day.
Later that evening, I had a biopsy.

1735
01:56:28.741 --> 01:56:32.075
I was sedated, but my wife,
who was there,

1736
01:56:32.286 --> 01:56:35.450
told me that when they viewed the cells
under a microscope,

1737
01:56:35.581 --> 01:56:38.289
the doctors started crying
because it turned out to be

1738
01:56:38.417 --> 01:56:42.752
a very rare form of pancreatic cancer
that is curable with surgery.

1739
01:56:42.880 --> 01:56:46.499
I had the surgery
and, thankfully, I'm fine now.

1740
01:56:50.137 --> 01:56:52.845
<i>Steve Jobs talked about
his cancer very emotionally</i>

1741
01:56:52.973 --> 01:56:56.557
<i>at the Stanford graduation ceremony
in 2005.</i>

1742
01:56:56.685 --> 01:56:58.677
And he very clearly told the story

1743
01:56:58.812 --> 01:57:01.350
to make it sound as though
he had been diagnosed

1744
01:57:01.524 --> 01:57:04.312
and moved immediately to surgery,
and been cured.

1745
01:57:04.443 --> 01:57:05.729
That simply wasn't true.

1746
01:57:05.861 --> 01:57:09.445
<i>What I found out, over a period
of months of reporting,</i>

1747
01:57:09.573 --> 01:57:12.316
<i>was that Steve Jobs actually
had been diagnosed with cancer</i>

1748
01:57:12.451 --> 01:57:16.195
<i>nine months earlier, and that
for a period of nine months,</i>

1749
01:57:16.372 --> 01:57:19.831
<i>he had refused to have the surgery
that every medical expert said</i>

1750
01:57:19.959 --> 01:57:22.622
<i>was necessary to increase
his prospects for survival.</i>

1751
01:57:22.753 --> 01:57:24.745
Instead of having the surgery,

1752
01:57:24.880 --> 01:57:28.590
he had sought
alternative medicine approaches

1753
01:57:28.717 --> 01:57:30.800
to try to cure himself of cancer.

1754
01:57:31.971 --> 01:57:34.714
<i>Entrepreneurs have
an almost pathological need</i>

1755
01:57:34.848 --> 01:57:36.760
<i>to control their own fate.</i>

1756
01:57:36.892 --> 01:57:38.178
<i>They'll take any suffering</i>

1757
01:57:38.310 --> 01:57:40.142
if they can just be in charge
of their destiny

1758
01:57:40.312 --> 01:57:42.304
and not have it
in somebody else's hands.

1759
01:57:42.439 --> 01:57:47.525
<i>Apple's stock has been
volatile on rumors about Jobs's health.</i>

1760
01:57:47.653 --> 01:57:49.861
The company's stock
was halted for a time,

1761
01:57:49.989 --> 01:57:53.699
then took a big hit when it re-opened
in after-hours trading.

1762
01:57:53.826 --> 01:57:55.783
<i>Apple's share prices have dropped</i>

1763
01:57:55.911 --> 01:57:58.824
<i>with every pound
that Jobs has lost in recent months.</i>

1764
01:58:00.207 --> 01:58:02.665
<i>Apple really landed
into a dicey situation</i>

1765
01:58:02.793 --> 01:58:04.625
<i>when Steve decided
to issue a letter saying,</i>

1766
01:58:04.753 --> 01:58:06.619
<i>"Well, you know,
nothing's wrong with me."</i>

1767
01:58:06.755 --> 01:58:09.247
"It's just a minor problem,"
And it wasn't a minor problem.

1768
01:58:10.676 --> 01:58:14.169
<i>Steve Jobs made a point
of withholding from the world</i>

1769
01:58:14.346 --> 01:58:16.804
<i>that he faced this illness.</i>

1770
01:58:16.932 --> 01:58:18.719
<i>That's something
investors want to know,</i>

1771
01:58:18.851 --> 01:58:20.558
<i>because when you
bought Apple stock then,</i>

1772
01:58:20.728 --> 01:58:22.640
you were buying into Steve Jobs.

1773
01:58:22.813 --> 01:58:26.227
He was obligated
to tell shareholders right away

1774
01:58:26.358 --> 01:58:27.849
about this serious illness.

1775
01:58:29.028 --> 01:58:31.736
<i>You do personally
want to give Steve the privacy,</i>

1776
01:58:31.864 --> 01:58:34.823
<i>but Steve put the spotlight on him,</i>

1777
01:58:34.950 --> 01:58:38.739
and you can't turn that off
just because it's inconvenient.

1778
01:58:41.624 --> 01:58:44.367
<i>After the story came out,
I saw Steve.</i>

1779
01:58:44.501 --> 01:58:47.084
He started talking about Apple,
and he said,

1780
01:58:47.212 --> 01:58:51.456
<i>"You know, Apple is really a company
that doesn't have any divisions."</i>

1781
01:58:51.592 --> 01:58:53.709
<i>"We don't have all this bureaucracy."</i>

1782
01:58:53.844 --> 01:58:56.757
And I said, "Steve, that would be
a great story for <i>Fortune."</i>

1783
01:58:56.889 --> 01:59:02.510
And he looked at me, and he said,
"No."

1784
01:59:02.645 --> 01:59:08.391
"I don't think we can do that story
with you. Not now."

1785
01:59:09.985 --> 01:59:11.601
"Not now."

1786
01:59:11.820 --> 01:59:15.734
And then he said,
and the whole room went quiet,

1787
01:59:15.866 --> 01:59:19.826
and he said, "you know, we used to..."

1788
01:59:21.163 --> 01:59:24.702
"We used to really be friends
with <i>Fortune,</i>

1789
01:59:24.833 --> 01:59:31.797
I used to be friends with <i>Fortune,</i>
but not anymore. Not anymore."

1790
01:59:31.924 --> 01:59:35.463
And then I remember these tears
came out of his eyes,

1791
01:59:35.594 --> 01:59:37.051
and one was on his glasses,

1792
01:59:37.179 --> 01:59:41.674
and then one, I remember it rolling
off his cheek and hitting his shirt,

1793
01:59:41.809 --> 01:59:46.850
and he was just crying,
and the whole room was silent.

1794
01:59:48.899 --> 01:59:52.609
Apple CEO Steve Jobs today
made his first public appearance

1795
01:59:52.736 --> 01:59:54.602
since getting a new liver
five months ago.

1796
01:59:54.738 --> 01:59:55.854
<i>For the first time,</i>

1797
01:59:55.989 --> 01:59:58.902
<i>the 54-year-old CEO
publicly acknowledged</i>

1798
01:59:59.034 --> 02:00:02.493
<i>the liver transplant this spring
that saved his life.</i>

1799
02:00:02.621 --> 02:00:08.242
Well, I now have the liver
of a mid-20s person.

1800
02:00:11.880 --> 02:00:16.500
I'd like to take a moment and thank
everybody in the Apple community

1801
02:00:16.635 --> 02:00:20.845
for the heartfelt support I got, too.
It really meant a lot.

1802
02:00:20.973 --> 02:00:25.843
And I'd also like
to especially thank Tim Cook

1803
02:00:25.978 --> 02:00:29.688
and the entire executive team of Apple.

1804
02:00:29.815 --> 02:00:32.228
They really rose to the occasion

1805
02:00:32.359 --> 02:00:36.478
and ran the company very ably
in that difficult period.

1806
02:00:36.613 --> 02:00:38.946
So, thank you, guys.
Let's give them a round of applause.

1807
02:00:40.492 --> 02:00:42.074
<i>He loved what he did.</i>

1808
02:00:42.202 --> 02:00:44.239
I do have an e-mail from him
saying that.

1809
02:00:44.371 --> 02:00:46.863
<i>He said, you know,
"Both of us were fortunate," he said,</i>

1810
02:00:46.999 --> 02:00:49.366
<i>in that we loved what we did</i>

1811
02:00:49.543 --> 02:00:52.286
<i>and we were able to do it
for a long time.</i>

1812
02:00:52.421 --> 02:00:54.834
<i>And he said,
"What else could you ask for?"</i>

1813
02:00:56.216 --> 02:01:00.927
We're maybe a little more experienced,
certainly more beat-up,

1814
02:01:01.054 --> 02:01:04.764
but the core values are the same.

1815
02:01:04.892 --> 02:01:09.512
I don't see why you have to change
if you get big.

1816
02:01:09.688 --> 02:01:11.896
<i>Straightforward to me.</i>

1817
02:01:12.024 --> 02:01:14.357
<i>Apple was big.</i>

1818
02:01:14.526 --> 02:01:18.816
<i>By this time, one of the biggest
corporations in the world.</i>

1819
02:01:21.283 --> 02:01:24.867
<i>But each time we saw Jobs,
he seemed smaller.</i>

1820
02:01:28.373 --> 02:01:32.287
<i>As his devices got stronger,
Jobs got weaker.</i>

1821
02:01:32.419 --> 02:01:35.912
It's so much more intimate
than a laptop,

1822
02:01:36.048 --> 02:01:39.291
and it's so much more capable
than a smartphone

1823
02:01:39.426 --> 02:01:41.258
with this gorgeous, large display.

1824
02:01:41.386 --> 02:01:45.175
<i>I think that a lot of the grief
at Jobs's death</i>

1825
02:01:45.307 --> 02:01:50.473
was a fear that we had been
very comfortable

1826
02:01:50.646 --> 02:01:52.979
<i>for the last decade in his hands.</i>

1827
02:01:53.106 --> 02:01:57.771
It's phenomenal to hold the internet
in your hand.

1828
02:01:57.903 --> 02:02:00.190
<i>That he was going to keep
doing these amazing things.</i>

1829
02:02:00.322 --> 02:02:04.282
Now he's gone, and there's no indication
that anyone's going to replace him.

1830
02:02:36.984 --> 02:02:38.725
Welcome.

1831
02:02:44.700 --> 02:02:46.066
Thank you.

1832
02:02:58.463 --> 02:03:00.170
Thank you very much.

1833
02:03:03.969 --> 02:03:05.551
- It always helps...
- We love you!

1834
02:03:05.721 --> 02:03:09.305
Thank you.

1835
02:03:09.516 --> 02:03:12.805
It always helps,
and I appreciate it very much.

1836
02:03:12.936 --> 02:03:16.850
<i>He resigned officially
in August. Two weeks earlier,</i>

1837
02:03:16.982 --> 02:03:20.475
<i>Apple had become the highest-valued
corporation on Earth.</i>

1838
02:03:20.652 --> 02:03:22.268
And thank you for coming so much.

1839
02:03:22.404 --> 02:03:24.987
<i>We've got a great week
planned for you.</i>

1840
02:03:49.932 --> 02:03:52.925
<i>This is a field
where one does not write a principia</i>

1841
02:03:53.060 --> 02:03:55.643
<i>which holds up for 200 years</i>

1842
02:03:55.771 --> 02:03:58.104
<i>or paints a painting that'll be
looked at for centuries,</i>

1843
02:03:58.231 --> 02:04:01.941
<i>or builds a church
that will be admired</i>

1844
02:04:02.069 --> 02:04:03.981
<i>and looked at in astonishment
for centuries.</i>

1845
02:04:04.112 --> 02:04:06.445
No, this is a field
where one does one's work,

1846
02:04:06.573 --> 02:04:08.485
and in ten years, it's obsolete

1847
02:04:08.617 --> 02:04:11.234
and really will not be useable
within ten or 20 years.

1848
02:04:11.370 --> 02:04:13.282
I mean, you can't go back
and use an Apple I,

1849
02:04:13.413 --> 02:04:14.574
cos there's no software for it.

1850
02:04:14.748 --> 02:04:17.206
In another ten years or so,
you won't be able to use an Apple II.

1851
02:04:17.334 --> 02:04:19.792
You won't even be able to fire it up
and see what it was like.

1852
02:04:21.546 --> 02:04:25.005
<i>It's sort of like
sediment of rocks.</i>

1853
02:04:25.133 --> 02:04:26.840
<i>You're building up a mountain,</i>

1854
02:04:26.969 --> 02:04:30.133
<i>and you get to contribute
your little layer of sedimentary rock</i>

1855
02:04:30.263 --> 02:04:32.255
<i>to make the mountain that much higher.</i>

1856
02:04:32.391 --> 02:04:35.975
<i>But no one on the surface will,
unless they have x-ray vision,</i>

1857
02:04:36.144 --> 02:04:37.851
<i>will see your sediment.</i>

1858
02:04:41.274 --> 02:04:44.688
<i>In Japan, there's
an idea called "mono no aware,"</i>

1859
02:04:44.820 --> 02:04:48.154
<i>meaning "the deep awareness of things".</i>

1860
02:04:48.281 --> 02:04:51.240
<i>It celebrates the melancholy
of the passing of life</i>

1861
02:04:51.368 --> 02:04:55.157
<i>and sees more beauty in the fallen leaf
than the one on the branch.</i>

1862
02:04:56.415 --> 02:04:59.328
<i>Maybe that's what Japan held for Jobs.</i>

1863
02:04:59.459 --> 02:05:04.329
<i>The sadness of the soul
as expressed in the beauty of things.</i>

1864
02:05:09.261 --> 02:05:11.878
<i>In the end, I was left
with the same question</i>

1865
02:05:12.014 --> 02:05:13.550
<i>with which I began this journey.</i>

1866
02:05:13.682 --> 02:05:18.222
<i>"Why did so many strangers weep
for Steve Jobs?"</i>

1867
02:05:18.353 --> 02:05:21.642
<i>It's too simple to say it was
because he gave us products we love</i>

1868
02:05:21.815 --> 02:05:25.684
<i>without asking why we love them
the way we do.</i>

1869
02:05:25.861 --> 02:05:28.069
<i>It's too simple even to conclude
that we love them</i>

1870
02:05:28.238 --> 02:05:30.355
<i>because they connect us
to a wider world</i>

1871
02:05:30.490 --> 02:05:33.608
<i>and the people in our lives
that are far away,</i>

1872
02:05:33.785 --> 02:05:37.119
<i>because these machines isolate us, too.</i>

1873
02:05:39.708 --> 02:05:43.577
<i>Perhaps the contradictory nature
of our experience with these gadgets</i>

1874
02:05:43.712 --> 02:05:46.295
<i>mirrors the contradictions
in Jobs himself.</i>

1875
02:05:48.675 --> 02:05:52.715
<i>He was an artist who sought perfection,
but could never find peace.</i>

1876
02:05:54.973 --> 02:05:58.842
<i>He had the focus of a monk,
but none of the empathy.</i>

1877
02:06:01.104 --> 02:06:04.814
<i>He offered us freedom,
but only within a closed garden,</i>

1878
02:06:04.941 --> 02:06:07.058
<i>to which he held the key.</i>

1879
02:06:11.323 --> 02:06:13.315
<i>To reconcile these contradictions,</i>

1880
02:06:13.450 --> 02:06:17.194
<i>I think we have to look to the other
half of our relationship with Jobs.</i>

1881
02:06:17.329 --> 02:06:18.991
<i>To ourselves.</i>

1882
02:06:22.250 --> 02:06:26.460
<i>As Jobs wanted it,
the screen of my iPhone is dark.</i>

1883
02:06:26.630 --> 02:06:29.213
<i>A Zen landscape of the unseen.</i>

1884
02:06:29.341 --> 02:06:34.302
<i>If I stare into it, I see
an obscure reflection of myself,</i>

1885
02:06:34.429 --> 02:06:37.046
<i>but this impression
lasts just a fleeting moment</i>

1886
02:06:37.182 --> 02:06:40.766
<i>before I press the home key
and the screen lights up.</i>

1887
02:06:40.936 --> 02:06:44.771
<i>But perhaps I should spend a moment
regarding that reflection,</i>

1888
02:06:44.940 --> 02:06:50.937
<i>asking myself what, in buying
and using this product, I am doing?</i>

1889
02:06:51.071 --> 02:06:53.563
<i>What is the full nature
of my transaction</i>

1890
02:06:53.740 --> 02:06:58.155
<i>with the maker of this magical
and intimate machine?</i>

1891
02:07:18.807 --> 02:07:24.974
<i>Once I wanted to be the greatest</i>

1892
02:07:25.105 --> 02:07:27.392
<i>Greatest, greatest, greatest</i>

1893
02:07:27.524 --> 02:07:33.361
<i>No wind or waterfall could stall me</i>

1894
02:07:34.781 --> 02:07:40.448
<i>And then came the rush of the flood</i>

1895
02:07:40.620 --> 02:07:45.832
<i>Stars at night turned deep to dust</i>

1896
02:07:46.001 --> 02:07:51.463
<i>Melt me down into big, black armor</i>

1897
02:07:54.551 --> 02:08:01.469
<i>Leave no trace of grace
Just in your honor</i>

1898
02:08:03.685 --> 02:08:09.397
<i>Lower me down to culprit south</i>

1899
02:08:09.524 --> 02:08:13.108
<i>Make 'em wash a space in town</i>

1900
02:08:13.236 --> 02:08:17.230
<i>For the lead and the dregs of my bed</i>

1901
02:08:17.365 --> 02:08:20.824
<i>I've been sleeping</i>

1902
02:08:20.994 --> 02:08:23.828
<i>Lower me down</i>

1903
02:08:23.997 --> 02:08:28.332
<i>Pin me in, secure the grounds</i>

1904
02:08:30.378 --> 02:08:31.994
<i>For the later parade</i>
